window.harnessChunks["rows-30f1f08648ab43d7.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:17:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">zeroshot</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot/tree/4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\">4c2ed7d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement user-requested code changes or run research experiments with model-directed tool/file actions and return artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: arbitrary codebase tasks, repository state, model/tool feedback, tests and experiment outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: instantiated worker/researcher agent selects substantive coding or experiment actions; zeroshot graph invokes and retains the role under a specific runtime binding. - Decision owner: the first-party configured graph node&#39;s autonomous agent role through a supported harness adapter; external harness internals retain lower-level execution but are not separately scored. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/harness binding, graph transitions, workspace/capsule, runner, provider session, SQLite ledger and bounded retries. - Closure path: task input → worker/researcher model/tool action → workspace artifact/experiment evidence → result returned to graph and, after review, revised worker action or accepted output. - Boundary reachability: zeroshot run --template software-change and auto-research are built-in supported native CLI/Docker graph modes with first-party runner and first-party role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph plumbing cannot implement semantic code changes or choose experiments without a model-driven worker; the autonomous operational actor is necessary. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; autoresearch.rs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between independently active run/postmortem cells that share a scoped lesson store and might overwrite contradictory or stale guidance. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing runs and their postmortem maintenance/discovery agents, each producing prospective repository/graph advice for the same shared memory scope. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent updates to a lesson from stale snapshots, resurrecting a retired lesson, incompatible parallel advice replacing a newer record and duplicated overlapping recommendations. - Attenuating coordination relation: each specialist returns keep/adapt/retire/defer for frozen lesson IDs; host requires expected revisions and atomic publication, rejects conflicting writes, reloads the latest lesson snapshot, then re-invokes model reconciliation rather than accepting last-writer-wins. Same-scope overlap is consolidated; unrelated scopes cannot rewrite each other. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a publication conflict suspends the stale mutation and returns refreshed data to maintenance agents; only successfully reconciled newer cards become injectable guidance into subsequent model/tool sessions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the evidence identifies actual competing independent agent/run modifications of one shared knowledge resource and a conflict-specific reject/reconcile/return path, not merely a datastore or graph edge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cross-run retained-guidance collisions and stale-write interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to reconcile a stale/overlapping scoped lesson so concurrent-run advice can coexist or supersede safely. - Decision owner: first-party optimistic-concurrency and scoped specialist reconciliation constructor; autonomous maintenance judgments are available but the general inter-run arbitration mode remains scoped/composed, not a universal S2 organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned SQLite transactions, tombstones, stable IDs, scoped snapshots, conflict detection, specialist result schema and refresh/retry budget. - Closure path: concurrent postmortem decisions → CAS/version conflict → refreshed scope + specialist reconsideration → atomic accepted lesson revisions → future run retrieval or explicit failed reconciliation. - Boundary reachability: this is the supported local/Docker Active Memory path described in guide, implemented in postmortem.rs and store.rs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party mechanism explicitly constructs a recoverable S2-specific path; it does not establish broadly agent-owned coordination of every simultaneous run, so the conservative publication is C. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; store.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: conflict witness is shared retained lessons across runs, not all inter-agent workflow interference. Worker/reviewer fanout and graph scheduling alone are not used as S2 proof.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: manage the current auto-research program and its experiment/review/correction choices across distinct scouting and research S1 units; not all unrelated zeroshot installations. - Whole-system current view: one auto-research run&#39;s three scout proposals, current experiment/report, reviewer verdict and feedback, retained candidate and bounded iteration state. - Current-control decision scope: select which current approach/experiment to execute, revise work from review evidence, retain accepted result or stop a blocked/inconclusive round. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing scout directions, experimental failure, reviewer rejection and incomplete current research commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven researcher chooses experiment and revision priorities from scouts and review, while native graph enforces maximum iterations and terminal decisions. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated researcher Agent in the built-in auto-research graph; graph scheduler supplies deterministic execution constraints, and fresh reviewer provides challenge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Par scout fan-in, researcher continuation session, bounded inner review loop, current state/feedback fields, SQLite run ledger and optional push gate. - Closure path: three model scout proposals → researcher decision/experiment → fresh reviewer evidence → same researcher revises or accepts → next round&#39;s current plan/retained result. - Boundary reachability: auto-research native template with shipped researcher instructions is built-in, not a user-authored hypothetical graph. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model researcher owns current experiment selection and corrective judgment; removing it leaves fixed fan-in/limits but not the substantive cross-scout control choice. - Evidence: README auto-research; autoresearch.rs; researcher.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: S3=A is specific to the built-in multi-agent research-program mode. In software-change, fixed graph control and reviewer gates alone are not credited as an independent model-driven S3 controller.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test the implementer&#39;s outcome through fresh independent acceptance/code reviewers, then force rework on rejection before delivery or successful closure. - Claim being audited: worker&#39;s code meets requested acceptance criteria and code-quality/correctness expectations, not merely that the worker completed a turn. - Ordinary reporting path: worker writes/edit files, returns output and claims completed implementation in its retained session. - Complementary access path: separate acceptance and code reviewer nodes run in parallel against the shared workspace, each with its own fresh model session and rubric; their structured verdict/diagnostic inputs are not copied from the worker&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: both reviewers start fresh on every pass while implementation/repair uses the same worker session; the native graph gives reviewer roles separate identities and returns both findings. - Who acts on findings: enforced graph routes rejected reviewer evidence back to original worker; the next pass repeats both independent reviewers. Delivery/CI conflicts may also return through controlled repair and re-review. Acceptance cannot bypass guards with a prose-only assertion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect self-reported success, acceptance gaps, code defects, regression and unreviewed delivery changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately model-driven reviewer decides accepted/rejected criteria and can prevent progression, triggering another worker repair round. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated independent reviewer/verifier agents for the standard software-change mode; trusted native graph gates/runner enforce their verdicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed graph signals, Par reviewer join, guard routing, bounded global loop budget, retained worker session, fresh reviewer sessions, run ledger and CI/delivery gates. - Closure path: worker code → fresh acceptance+code review → accepted or rejection diagnostics → worker repair → new review pass → accepted artifact/delivery or bounded honest failure. - Boundary reachability: the default software-change template and review/repair continuity tests demonstrate a standard operational path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers independently choose substantive feedback; graph machinery supplies independent session enforcement and corrective return, not semantic acceptance decisions itself. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; continuity.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: independence is of decision sessions/evidence roles, not guaranteed model-provider diversity or exhaustive correctness. User-specified graph/model settings can alter actual review coverage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: discover and reconcile costly recurring errors and outdated procedural knowledge so later graph/repository agent work is better adapted to changed evidence. - External distinction: real repository files and tool receipts, repeated costly failures/rejected review loops, current scope content and changed advice provenance, rather than the just-finished worker&#39;s optimistic summary. - Future / prospective distinction: determine which recurring or newly learned guidance will remain useful across subsequent runs, adapt stale lessons to current source and retire obsoleted/conflicting advice. - Adaptation option generated: graph and repository discovery specialists propose new procedural cards; separate maintenance specialists keep/adapt/retire/defer all supplied lessons, consolidate overlaps and identify stable recommendations. - Path back into current capability / S3: versioned accepted cards are stored per graph/node/repository and injected through native pre-tool hooks or graph instructions in future sessions, changing model choices in later S1/S3 work without rewriting the authored graph or policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repetition of expensive mistakes, stale memory, changed code/graph context and loss of useful experience between runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maintenance/discovery model specialists judge evidence-backed guidance changes; host validates cost/provenance, revisions and scope before publication. - Decision owner: standard-distribution postmortem model specialists in separate sessions, automatically enabled by default when Active Memory scopes and postmortem remain enabled; fixed validation/SQLite machinery enforces their decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool receipt capture, post-run outcome latch, per-scope indexes, strict cost threshold, version/CAS, bounded context, admission validation, tombstones, retrieval budgets and native hooks. - Closure path: run evidence/tool receipts → postmortem specialist review/discovery → validated revised scoped lessons → durable store → future run retrieval/injection → later model work and next postmortem reassessment. - Boundary reachability: the built-in Active Memory guide explicitly documents default enabled local/Docker paths; postmortem.rs, native supervisor, retrieval and hooks are source-backed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specialist agents exercise context-sensitive keep/adapt/retire/discover discretion from actual evidence; host-only ranking/CAS cannot independently decide what lesson remains useful for future work. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; reconciliation tests; hooks.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: postmortem may abstain when evidence is inadequate, can fail independently of completed main graph, and requires working model/harness hooks. Evidence of a closed adaptive mechanism is not proof that a given lesson causally improves future task quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate ultimate-identity/policy conflict adjudicator and authoritative return loop exists within a graph-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime scopes, model choices, graph contracts, delivery authorization and user approvals are fixed constraints rather than identity-level questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent or parent actor is first-party delegated ultimate purpose/identity choice at this recursion. - Decision owner: user/operator defines graph, tasks, credentials, publishing authority and scope; native runtime enforces these settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph validation, authored runtime plan, capsules, limits, run ledger, Git/CI policy and memory namespace controls. - Closure path: an operator can change configuration for later runs, but no outstanding identity-policy matter is routed to a legitimate S5 authority and returned through an explicit governance closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph profile names, model persona, user delivery ratification, or managed platform policy do not by themselves constitute identity or ultimate-policy decisions. - Evidence: README; graph reference; Active Memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec/assessments/ze/zeroshot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec\">0f8636f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:33:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Swarm</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm/tree/65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\">65e0ab6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Operational delivery by distinct coding/research/review workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended implementation/research requests, uncertain tool results and task failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose task-local investigation, tool actions, implementation and completion/progress response. - Decision owner: The model-driven operational worker in the configured provider session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runner prompt assembly, provider adapters, MCP tools, task persistence and runtime limits transport/enforce worker decisions. - Closure path: Task → assembled context → worker tool/action choices → progress/result → task and lead follow-up. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model worker leaves scheduling/storage, not the same discretionary investigation or implementation. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; src/commands/runner.ts (adapter.createSession and task handling); templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Provider access is required; the assessment does not import unexamined provider governance or claim successful business output.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among concurrent S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New requests arrive through different ingresses while related work remains in flight, risking duplicate or contradictory implementation/review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret sibling intent, coordinate with named workers, avoid duplicate action, and choose compatible task work. - Decision owner: Lead and operational model workers own the coordination judgment; the sibling detector does not decide task semantics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: contextKey lookup, in-flight task status, original-intent rendering, messaging/task tools, same-agent parent-session selection and lead no-duplicate rules. - Closure path: Same-context sibling discovery → task prompt lists owners/status/original requests and instructs coordinate/do not duplicate → agent queries/messages/reuses ongoing work → subsequent assignment/action changes. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sibling context intentionally exposes this inter-worker disturbance; the agent decides what overlaps and what should change. - Evidence: src/tasks/sibling-awareness.ts; src/tasks/sibling-block.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md rules 2, 7, 8; src/tools/send-task.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Structural disturbance witness, not a claim that every collision is prevented. Incorrect/missing context keys can miss related work. - Distinct S1 units: Separately assigned coder, researcher and reviewer workers in the official multi-worker deployment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent same-context tasks from multiple ingresses can duplicate or conflict with each other&#39;s unfinished work; same-PR reviews can be double-assigned. - Attenuating coordination relation: Named sibling tasks and status are supplied to the new worker; explicit coordinate/do-not-duplicate instructions plus task inspection/messaging let it adjust alongside lead deduplication. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Coordination changes which work a new task performs and whether lead creates/routes another task; same-agent continuation can resume existing context. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party renderer explicitly detects concurrent related work, describes its original intent and instructs interference attenuation. Generic mailboxes or dependsOn alone are not the witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the team&#39;s inside-and-now commitments and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled sessions, failed workers, unclaimed recovery pins, affinity-starved tasks and abandoned commitments across the team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assess current state and choose reroute, retry, cancel, recreate or intervention rather than blindly continue. - Decision owner: The official lead agent owns discretionary current-control decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Heartbeat preflight and code triage detect/escalate; snapshots, task creation, steer/cancel/send tools enforce the selected decision. - Closure path: Whole-team status and standing orders → lead heartbeat/reroute-decision task or worker-failure follow-up → lead investigates and chooses corrective task/resource action → task service changes subsequent worker operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic retry thresholds and transactions expose exceptional state; lead must choose the organizational response after those mechanisms cannot settle it. - Evidence: src/heartbeat/heartbeat.ts; src/heartbeat/templates.ts; templates/official/lead/HEARTBEAT.md; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts task.worker.failed; src/tools/steer-task.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: A parent can administer deployment, but isolated configuration or approval is not counted as a distinct evidenced parent S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: Gathered agents, active/pending/failed tasks and stalled sessions, combined with lead standing orders and full task-detail access. - Current-control decision scope: Team commitments and recovery: cancel obsolete work, reassign eligible workers, steer current execution, recreate still-needed failed work and avoid retry storms.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary audit of an operational code-delivery claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coder can report completion while code contains security/race/state-machine faults or inadequate evidence/tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Form an independent review judgment with blocking findings/request-changes and return corrective requirements. - Decision owner: A distinct official reviewer model session owns audit judgment; coder/lead own correction and subsequent work routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate worker environment/task, direct git checkout, GitHub review/CI access, task completion/follow-up and parentTaskId continuity. - Closure path: Lead routes a review to reviewer → reviewer independently clones/checks out actual PR code and examines tests/CI → blocking findings/request changes → coder resolves feedback with parentTaskId continuity; lead can route corrective work. Human approval remains a separate merge requirement. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer directly inspects operational evidence in a separate role/session and decides substantive findings, beyond the implementer&#39;s summary or deterministic CI verdict. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; templates/official/reviewer/CLAUDE.md; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md routing/review rules; templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md review-feedback rule; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: This is a supported role-based audit path when lead assigns review, not proof every task is audited. The ordinary 500-character completion follow-up alone is not complementary audit; shared credentials/model family are not institutional independence. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s proposed change is ready/correct and tests cover changed paths. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder progress/completion and a summary follow-up task to lead. - Complementary access path: Reviewer must clone repository and check out the PR branch, inspect actual code, tests and CI; not only read the coder&#39;s summary/diff. - Independence boundary: Separate reviewer worker/session and instructions, distinct from the producing coder; official deployment includes the reviewer. - Who acts on findings: Reviewer requests changes with concrete blocking fixes; coder resolves review feedback in the originating task context, with lead routing and subsequent review as needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Outside-and-then intelligence for the deployed team&#39;s own GTM campaign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing discovery channels, search demand and content opportunities make the team&#39;s existing GTM plan less effective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Turn external trend distinctions into candidate future campaign/content adaptations; adopter must still compose selection and implementation closure. - Decision owner: The configured lead produces options; deployment owner must supply campaign goal, integrations and the decisive adoption/return-to-S3 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party parameterized weekly schedule prompt, GitHub/GSC integrations, configured GTM plan/state, admin report and saved research workspace. - Closure path: Configured own-campaign metrics → week-over-week search/referrer/content analysis → what-to-try-next and gap/opportunity options → report. Turning that report into an adopted current-capability change is intentionally not supplied as an automatic closed mode. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A function-specific first-party prospective adaptation constructor exists, but no closed agent-owned or parent-owned adoption loop is established by the template. - Evidence: templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/config.json; templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/content.md; templates/official/discoverability-optimizer/SOUL.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Optional template; requires own GTM goal, REPOURL/GSCPROPERTY and integrations. Ordinary customer research as a delivered task, generic memory, persona edits and arbitrary extensions are not S4 witnesses. - External distinction: Repository traffic/referrers/popular paths and Search Console queries, impressions, CTR and rankings for the deployment&#39;s configured campaign. - Future / prospective distinction: Week-over-week change, high-impression low-CTR/rank opportunities, content gaps and what to try next against the current GTM plan. - Adaptation option generated: Candidate SEO/content/campaign experiments to improve the team&#39;s own future reach. - Path back into current capability / S3: Report through configured admin/in-app channels and saved research workspace intentionally exposes options to the deployment; actor authority and adoption/execution closure must be composed, so C rather than A/P.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity and ultimate-policy adjudication at deployed-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A prospective conflict about the team&#39;s constitutive purpose or ultimate policy would require authoritative settlement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive identity/ultimate-policy arbitration and return loop is supplied in the inspected deployed surfaces. - Decision owner: No in-boundary S5 owner established; operator configuration and lead profile editing are not sufficient evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL/IDENTITY/CLAUDE persistence, update-profile, repo guidelines, permission/budget and extension activation controls. - Closure path: Observed paths persist persona, operational checks and permissions; they do not reconstruct a disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter being authoritatively settled and governing future operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither writable identity files nor final human say on individual merges establishes the S5 function. - Evidence: docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/architecture/agents.mdx/architecture/agents.mdx); src/tools/update-profile.ts; src/tools/repos/update-repo.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts; docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/guides/extensions.mdx/guides/extensions.mdx). - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Bounded absence in this deployed distribution, not an impossibility claim for a separately composed organization&#39;s governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7/assessments/ag/agent-swarm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7\">5484791 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T03:07:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gas Town</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown/tree/649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\">649b832 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded software-engineering issues in project repositories and return completed branch/work results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository state, implementation choices, tool/test observations, local blockers and discovered defects within an assigned issue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive implementation/research/debugging actions required to satisfy the hooked issue and decide when the bounded work is ready to submit through the prescribed completion path. - Decision owner: the model-driven Polecat worker agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated git worktrees, hooked Beads assignments, role/formula context, tool-capable model runtime, direct issue state updates and gt done completion plumbing. - Closure path: Mayor/runtime hooks an issue to a Polecat → Polecat executes autonomously in its isolated worktree → commits/pushes and runs the completion protocol → branch/result enters the downstream Witness/Refinery or configured PR path. - Boundary reachability: Polecat role templates, worktree provisioning, hook state and completion commands are shipped first-party Gas Town surfaces at the pinned revision; the external model host supplies inference but does not replace Gas Town&#39;s first-party worker contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role and formula constrain execution, but the model-driven worker chooses substantive code/tool actions and how to solve the assigned issue. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/polecat.md.tmpl, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate integration interference among independently produced Polecat branches so concurrent work does not collide on a stale common baseline or land in an incompatible order. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple autonomous workers can finish branches from earlier target states; parallel/direct merging can create conflicts, invalid dependency order or inconsistent integration as the target branch moves after each accepted change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next branch/merge order using priority, dependencies and timing; rebase it against the current target; resolve or reject conflicts; and require each subsequent branch to rebase against the newly advanced target. - Decision owner: the model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-rig merge queue, MERGEREADY protocol, isolated worker branches, sequential-rebase rule, target-resolution rules and merge-request state. - Closure path: multiple Polecat outputs enter the queue → Refinery selects one and rebases it on the current target → accepted merge advances the target → the next queued branch is rebased against that new baseline; unresolvable conflicts reopen the source issue and return through Witness for new work. - Boundary reachability: the canonical Refinery role and merge queue are shipped first-party operating surfaces for non-fork rigs; the role contract explicitly assigns merge/conflict/order judgment to the running agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/state machinery exposes and enforces the integration surface, but the first-party Refinery contract gives the model agent discretion over ordering and conflict/failure disposition; this is not merely authored static sequencing. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/refinery/batch.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: isolated worktrees by themselves would only be supporting interference attenuation; the A classification rests on the agent-owned sequential integration feedback loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more autonomous Polecats completing separate issues/branches in the same rig or integration target. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their branches can be based on the same earlier target and therefore conflict, invalidate each other&#39;s assumptions or require dependency-sensitive ordering when integrated. - Attenuating coordination relation: Refinery serially selects, rebases and integrates branches against the current moving target instead of allowing parallel direct merges. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: failed/conflicting work reopens its source issue and returns through Witness for re-sling/rework; successful integration changes the baseline against which later queued work is evaluated. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited relation addresses a concrete interference mode among outputs of independent S1 workers — incompatible concurrent branch integration — and feeds conflict/failure results back into later operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the town&#39;s current work commitments, dispatch, cross-rig allocation, escalations and active execution on behalf of the whole Gas Town workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unassigned ready work, stalled or failed workers, competing/cross-rig work, blocked dependencies, changed priorities, integration/escalation problems and idle or misallocated execution capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what work to dispatch and to which rig/worker, coordinate batch/cross-rig work, resolve escalations that Witnesses cannot handle, redirect priorities/integration decisions and intervene when current agents or rigs are stuck. - Decision owner: the model-driven Mayor agent in the standard autonomous mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: town/rig status, convoys, Beads dependencies, mail/escalation state, Polecat lists, hooks, gt sling, scheduler capacity support and Witness/Deacon feedback. - Closure path: current town/rig/convoy/escalation state becomes visible to Mayor → Mayor makes dispatch/coordination/intervention decisions → Gas Town creates/hooks workers or routes instructions/escalations → Polecat/Witness/Refinery/Deacon behavior proceeds under the changed current state and reports back. - Boundary reachability: Gas Town ships a dedicated Mayor session, first-party Mayor role template and control commands; Mayor is launched as an agent and connected to the same persisted work/communication surfaces used by the operational organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduler and hook mechanisms constrain execution, but the global dispatch, cross-rig judgment and escalation-resolution rights are assigned to the model-driven Mayor rather than being fully predetermined by runtime code. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, docs/design/escalation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the human Overseer can interact with or administratively operate Gas Town, but the reviewed evidence does not establish a sufficiently explicit distinct parent S3 operating mode comparable to a first-party approval/override lifecycle; ordinary human tasking and process controls are therefore not encoded as A(P). - Whole-system current view: Mayor has first-party commands and persisted state for overall town status, rigs, Polecats, active convoys, issue/dependency readiness, mail and escalations across the workspace. - Current-control decision scope: current work dispatch, worker/rig routing, batch/cross-rig coordination, escalation resolution, priority/integration judgments and intervention on stuck execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a Polecat&#39;s completion claim before its branch is allowed to land and return corrective action when the integrated result is not acceptable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-verification can miss failures that appear only after rebasing onto the current target, integration conflicts, branch-caused test failures or untracked pre-existing failures that would otherwise be merged without an independent gate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: run the configured verification suite after rebase, decide whether failure is branch-caused or pre-existing, block/reject a failing branch, reopen its source issue and require returned rework, or allow a verified branch to merge. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MERGEREADY handoff from Witness, separate Refinery session/role, queue state, rebase onto current target, configured setup/typecheck/lint/build/test commands, mandatory handle-failures gate and MERGEFAILED/MERGED return protocol. - Closure path: Polecat completion passes through Witness → Refinery obtains the branch independently and rebases/tests it → pass permits merge; branch-caused failure reopens source issue, closes the merge request and notifies Witness → Witness/current-control machinery can re-sling corrected operational work. - Boundary reachability: the verification gate is embedded in the shipped canonical Refinery patrol contract and directly controls whether standard non-fork work reaches the target branch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tests provide evidence but do not decide the organizational response; Refinery independently interprets conflict/failure provenance and owns the block/reopen/merge judgment under a role explicitly separated from application development. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/templates/roles/witness.md.tmpl. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the same Refinery actor also owns S2 integration ordering, but the S3 claim uses a distinct decision right: independent acceptance/challenge of a producer completion claim and corrective return, not merge sequencing itself. - Claim being audited: that a Polecat&#39;s submitted branch is integration-safe and satisfies the configured quality/test checks sufficiently to land on the target branch. - Ordinary reporting path: Polecat performs its own completion/self-verification and signals done; Witness processes completion/lifecycle state and sends MERGEREADY. - Complementary access path: Refinery independently checks the queued branch after rebasing onto the current target and runs the configured verification commands in its own merge-processing session. - Independence boundary: Refinery is a separate agent role and is explicitly forbidden from acting as the application developer; it does not simply accept the producer&#39;s self-report and can block landing/reopen work. - Who acts on findings: Refinery directly blocks or merges; on failure it reopens the source issue and notifies Witness, which can return the work to a new/current operational worker.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party external/prospective adaptation function is established at the reviewed town boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external ecosystem change, future project/runtime conditions, model/provider evolution and strategic capability adaptation were inspected, but no qualifying shipped S4 owner and closed return loop was found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that continuously or episodically distinguishes outside/future conditions, develops adaptation options and returns a selected organizational adaptation into present Gas Town capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mayor strategic/integration judgments, shared memories, project/research documents, HOP/Gas City material, scheduler state and historical capability/ledger data can inform work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: no qualifying outside/future sensing → adaptation option → present capability/current-control return path is established in the credited standard operating surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Mayor&#39;s use of the word strategic concerns current architecture, priority and integration decisions in the reviewed role contract; without a distinct external/prospective sensing-and-adaptation loop it is not promoted to S4. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, reviewed design/research surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party organizational identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the declared Gas Town workspace boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user intent, architecture/priorities, role templates, directives/configuration and Overseer interaction were inspected, but no qualifying town-level identity dispute/policy authority with a closed S5 return path was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for choosing or ratifying the ultimate purpose/identity/constitutional policy of the Gas Town organization as distinct from assigning work, configuring roles or exercising current operational control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the human Overseer, Mayor interface, role/formula templates, project configuration, directives/overlays, rig lifecycle controls and user instructions shape the system but are not sufficient S5 ownership by themselves. - Closure path: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision lifecycle comparable to goal/constitution/strategy ratification and return into autonomous operation was found at the pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the human as Overseer, routing strategic questions to Mayor, or allowing an operator to configure/stop/dock agents does not by itself establish S5; those paths are tasking, configuration or current control unless the ultimate organizational identity decision is explicit. - Evidence: internal/cmd/mayor.go, internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/templates/roles/deacon.md.tmpl, reviewed configuration/directive surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624/assessments/gastown.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624\">7e14b6d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:14:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Octomind</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind/tree/fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\">fceb8cb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user objectives through model-directed local/MCP tool actions in an adaptive session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source tree, changing command results, failed tests, tool errors, external API responses and task-specific observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses the next tool, argument and work step after receiving prior action output. - Decision owner: first-party specialist actor in Octomind&#39;s session/chat runtime; the selected external model supplies inference, not a separate vendor harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: native Rust chat session, MCP call dispatch, role/tool config, session history, guardrails, compression, supervision and provider client. - Closure path: task→session model request→model tool instruction→MCP/native executor→recorded tool result→subsequent model turn or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: ordinary installed CLI and piped/daemon session modes enter same first-party model/tool session path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action choices depend on model reasoning about live observations, rather than being only a prewritten deterministic script; policy and tool mediation constrain choices but do not invent them. - Evidence: src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/session/chat/response/toolexecution.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, README.md. - Basis: source-backed installed autonomous specialist. - Confidence: high for runnable first-party code structure, not model task performance. - Caveats: tap-specific tools, hosted model weights and endpoint success are not part of ownership attribution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately demonstrated first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation relation within the selected coding-session and optional specialist group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: nested/dynamic agents can contend for model capacity or shared files; concrete conflicts are plausible but not shown as a closed native feedback relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inter-agent conflict-specific arbitration that changes affected S1 behavior after detecting interference; not established by inspected paths. - Decision owner: supervisor handles the whole session&#39;s plan; child delegation and MCP orchestration communicate tasks/results without proven distinctive inter-S1 collision arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate, MCP agent invocation, monitored tool processes, role constraints, generic authorizer/cost bounds and session/workdir state. - Closure path: child task/return and supervisor&#39;s whole-current plan changes do not independently form an S2-specific conflicting-worker attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a plan owner, tool batch authorization, per-role access bounds or generic delegation do not prove shared-resource contention detection and corrective return between separate operational divisions. - Evidence: src/supervisor/delegate.rs, src/mcp/agent/functions.rs, src/mcp/orchestration/monitor.rs, src/supervisor/plan.rs. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: moderate at selected recursion level. - Caveats: additional externally configured multi-agent topology might bring S2 evidence, but that would require a concrete first-party collision-specific path and return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: separate model-based whole-current supervisor of active specialist plan/phase commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled work, unverified phase completion, broken assumptions or changed trajectory within a multi-phase objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: external supervisor decides when to create, advance, hold or revise the operative phase plan according to runtime action evidence and original user outcome constraints. - Decision owner: first-party out-of-band supervisor model planner; runtime validates and applies its structured decision. The specialist does not possess the plan mutation tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan signals, per-turn preparation, callsupervisorjson, PlanDecision, sidecarstart/advance/revise, injected authoritative plan notes and bounded error latches. - Closure path: runtime-observed action/phase evidence→external planner decision→persistent current plan transition or evidence-grounded hold→runtime-plan note to model-directed specialist→subsequent task decisions occur under revised current phase. - Boundary reachability: first-party supervisor plan is enabled in shipped default config for qualifying complex tasks; trivial/answer-only jobs intentionally bypass it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model decides actual whole-task phase regulation and returned current commitments, not a fixed progress counter or human-only approval. A concerns this documented current-control mode only. - Evidence: src/supervisor/plan.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Whole-system current view: standing instructions, original task, active plan, phase trajectory, evidence ledger and outcome conditions, beyond a single pending model tool call. - Current-control decision scope: choose and revise current task phase commitments and prevent unjustified advancement despite specialist claims. - Basis: source-confirmed distinct autonomous supervisor decision/return. - Confidence: high for structural ownership and default enablement, without live demonstration. - Caveats: planner intentionally declines simple requests, failed model calls can leave bookkeeping unchanged, and no autonomy is imported from a third-party agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: out-of-band supervisor-model verification of producer specialist&#39;s claimed done/completion against independent runtime action evidence and artifacts, with returned corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, missing tests, unsupported work claims and regressions hidden by optimistic specialist narration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately tasked verifier determines Pass/Gaps/Indeterminate, with challenged findings able to prevent verified completion and re-enter correction. - Decision owner: distinct Octomind supervisor verification model invocation (not the specialist&#39;s own status token); first-party runtime owns the binding verdict gate and reentry budget. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime action ledger, readback output, task/claim, ground-truth Git state, structured decision schema, independent refutation, bounded reentry and learning outcome labels. - Closure path: specialist self-reports done→first-party runtime captures actions/artifacts→separate supervisor verifier judges them→Gaps returned to specialist for bounded correction or explicit unverified outcome; Pass finalizes verifiable claim/plan and logs verified outcome. - Boundary reachability: enabled supervisor/gate in packaged default TOML, called at qualifying actual completion claims in the session executor; not off-box CI or benchmark scoring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model performs substantive correctness judgment with access to runtime-owned evidence not controlled by producer, rather than mere transcript logging or same-agent self-review. - Evidence: src/supervisor/gate.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/supervisor/detect.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Claim being audited: requested coding task is actually completed and outcome conditions hold. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist done token, user-facing summary and tool outputs. - Complementary access path: separate supervisor builds task/evidence packet from session action ledger, Git state and tool readbacks; can independently request missing output. - Independence boundary: independent role/model call and runtime-furnished ledger rather than specialist self-report, though same configured model-provider infrastructure may serve both. - Who acts on findings: runtime applies completion gate, new specialist correction prompt or unverified handoff; successful gate finalizes plan and supplies outcome credit. - Basis: source-confirmed agent decision and binding first-party corrective return. - Confidence: high for wired audit constructor, live model quality untested. - Caveats: abstention/timeout is not evidence of passing; no claim that an independent reviewer actually ran in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: when operator enables optional evolution, external and cross-session observations are synthesized into proposed changes of future-session skills/guardrails; first-party counterfactual evaluation determines trial/promotion/retirement of generated behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurrent user constraints, observed repeated failure patterns, newly learned project rules and changed usefulness/cost of optional capabilities across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate supervisor model may autonomously synthesize an advisory skill candidate from grounded lessons; a first-party counterfactual trial judges whether activating it improves future outcomes, promotes an effective candidate or retires weak/regressed behavior. Parent controls enablement, effectful trials and explicit approval/reject/rollback. - Decision owner: detached learning supervisor plus native promotion/pruning policy own the autonomous S4 path; operator supplies initial opt-in and effectful authorizations, so supplementary parent (P) is explicitly recorded. User approval is not confused with autonomous model judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source-quote and outcome-grounded Lesson, per-project store + scope, synthesizeafterextraction/synthesizestoreifdue, schema verification/replay checks, candidate registry, Shadow→Trial→Active/Retired, held-out counterfactual control/treatment, initforsession and generated skill/guardrail runtime merges. - Closure path: verified previous-session evidence→deduped reusable lessons→separate supervisor chooses a capability candidate→native schema/replay validation→shadow sample of future tasks without application→gated live treatment with counterfactual results→promoted skill/guard accessible to new sessions (or retired candidate)→actual future behavior changes and continues receiving evidence. - Boundary reachability: this is installed first-party [supervisor.learning.evolution] enabled = true mode, off by default, with code from extraction to runtime activation; no unpublished external R&amp;D process or user-installed extension is required. Effectful guards need explicit operator authorization; advisory promotion can be autonomous once opted in. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective hypothesis/capability selection is produced by a model outside an individual current-task coding loop, then evaluated and fed back into later operational capabilities. It is not mere text-only memory recall or compaction. Both autonomous and parent-governed decisions have distinct documented roles. - Evidence: src/supervisor/learning/evolution/synthesize.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/registry.rs, src/supervisor/learning/extract.rs, src/session/context.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - External distinction: observations gathered across past sessions and project/role boundaries rather than an in-flight same task&#39;s tool result. - Future / prospective distinction: choose whether a machine-generated reusable skill/guard should persist as available behavior for later tasks. - Adaptation option generated: scope-bounded, verified native skill/guardrail candidate with a registry identity and explicit artifact path. - Path back into current capability / S3: runtime initforsession loads active generated skills and merges validated guardrails; promotion/retirement changes subsequent specialist behavior while gate/plan continue regulating current work. The operator may approve effectful candidates or revert the registry state. - Basis: first-party optional autonomous skill-generation→trial→activation and bounded parent rights. - Confidence: medium-high for source-confirmed pipeline; actual learning benefit not measured. - Caveats: evolution disabled in default config; without activation/grounded lessons or enough verified samples no positive rollout occurs. Distinguish confirmed code path from successful on-device trial. Parent permission for effectful native behaviors is mandatory.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimately constituted ultimate identity and organizational-purpose policymaker beyond scoped user/role constraints within an Octomind session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool misuse, role conflicts and evolved guardrail risks are addressed by operator instructions, bounded security policy and optional supervisor authorizer but do not constitute a separate S5 mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret and resolve top-level organizational identity, constitutional purpose and ultimate contested policy with binding return to S3/S4; not established. - Decision owner: external operator provides objectives/configuration, first-party security controller enforces those constraints, supervisor authorizer may optionally judge specific tool intent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role settings, hard guards, user-intent authorizer, command confirmation, generated-effectful authorization and user approval. - Closure path: a disallowed tool can be blocked, but no separately chartered ultimate organization policy/identity judgment returns to govern system aims; parent S4 guard approval does not create one. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy-as-code and delegated parent permissions are not sufficient to demonstrate a legitimately differentiated S5 above the task supervisor. - Evidence: src/supervisor/authorizer.rs, src/session/guardrails.rs, src/config/mod.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user or adopting organization might own external S5, but that is not a first-party installed control loop at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f/assessments/octomind.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f\">ae89fd4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:09:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blade Code</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code/tree/30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\">30f8684 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering and automation work through model-selected repository/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/test/browser failures, model/provider variation, context pressure and bounded delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret returned evidence, decide when to delegate or create a team and decide when the assigned operational objective is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Blade Code agent and, for delegated bounded work, first-party subagent/team-member agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/executor, permissions, context/session runtime, provider adapters, task admission, goal state, worktrees and cancellation. - Closure path: user/parent goal → agent chooses direct or delegated coding action → first-party runtime executes → tool/subagent/team evidence returns → agent revises work → completion is accepted when runtime completion gates permit it. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/Web/Headless/ACP runtime uses the same first-party Agent/tool machinery; Task and Team tools are shipped model-callable surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and constrains actions, but the model chooses open-ended implementation, investigation, delegation and completion decisions. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/Agent.ts; packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/task/task.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permissions and scheduling can delay/block actions without becoming the owner of the engineering decision itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrent operational teammates by assigning dependencies/ownership and isolating write-capable workstreams while coordinating peer progress. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate task claiming, dependency-order violations, concurrent workers editing overlapping workspace state, and peer work that must be synchronized before downstream tasks proceed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the team members, task partition, dependency graph, initial assignments and whether work benefits from parallel specialization/peer review; use peer messages to adjust coordination as execution unfolds. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamCreate/SendMessage, with deterministic task-graph/worktree mechanisms enforcing the selected relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamTaskGraph, atomic claim, automatic unblock, durable peer mailbox, managed worktree isolation for write-capable members and worktree lifecycle/merge machinery. - Closure path: lead identifies separable/interdependent work → creates roles/tasks/dependencies → teammates claim only unblocked work and write-capable members default to isolated worktrees → completion/unblocking/messages update peer execution → downstream units proceed under the adjusted coordination state. - Boundary reachability: TeamCreate, TeamTaskClaim, SendMessage and managed teammate worktrees are shipped first-party runtime tools, not documentation-only concepts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces claims, dependencies and isolation, but the lead agent chooses the substantive organizational partition and dependency structure. - Distinct S1 units: persistent model-backed teammates/subagents that own bounded implementation or review outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent workers can duplicate work, violate ordering, or collide through shared writes; managed worktrees and dependency/claim rules exist specifically to reduce those interactions. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead-selected DAG/assignment plus atomic task claiming, automatic dependency unblocking, durable peer messaging and worktree isolation for writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: completed tasks unblock dependent work; peer messages enter running teammate turns; task ownership/claim state changes which unit may work next; isolated results are later integrated rather than concurrently overwriting the same checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanisms regulate concrete interaction among simultaneously viable operational workers rather than merely transporting a delegated prompt. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamTaskGraph.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/SubagentWorktreeLifecycle.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: atomic claiming and worktree mechanics are deterministic support; autonomy is credited to the lead&#39;s model-owned organizational decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a live whole-team view and regulate current operational commitments while the multi-agent organization is running. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled or unwanted teammates, changed current priorities, task/member status changes, blocked downstream work and a team that should be stopped after synthesis or failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the live team/member/task graph, steer a running teammate with a direct message, broadcast a changed instruction, and terminate running teammates by deleting the current team. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamStatus, SendMessage and TeamDelete. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSessionStore-backed live member state, durable task graph, TeamMailbox, running-turn message delivery and team/member cancellation. - Closure path: lead inspects TeamStatus/current graph → detects a current execution need → sends targeted/broadcast steering or terminates the team → running teammate/session state changes → later TeamStatus/results reflect the intervention and the lead proceeds accordingly. - Boundary reachability: TeamStatus/SendMessage/TeamDelete are shipped model-callable first-party tools available to the team lead. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: live state storage and cancellation transport are deterministic, but the lead model interprets whole-current state and decides the substantive intervention. - Whole-system current view: TeamStatus returns live member state from AgentSessionStore together with durable task-graph state for the owned team. - Current-control decision scope: current peer steering, task/role supervision through the shared graph, and cancellation of running team commitments when work should stop. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamRuntime.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamMailbox.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic stop/cancel would not be enough alone; the positive claim rests on the combined live whole-team status surface plus targeted running-turn steering and commitment termination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge qualifying implementation work with a fresh read-only verifier whose evidence can block completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect implementation claims, failing tests/types/lint, medium/high-risk defects, race/security issues and author self-verification bias. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently execute allowed verification commands, inspect the authoritative changed-file scope and emit structured PASS/FAIL/PARTIAL findings. - Decision owner: the separately instantiated built-in verification agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh subagent context, read-only tool restrictions, authoritative verification prompt, mutation-revision tracking, bounded retry gate and deterministic completion policy. - Closure path: main agent changes qualifying implementation → runtime requires fresh verification Task → independent verifier gathers direct project/tool evidence and emits verdict → FAIL/PARTIAL forces repair/re-verification; only fresh PASS for current mutation revision permits completion. - Boundary reachability: independentVerification is wired into the shipped main loop and explicitly requires the built-in verification subagent for qualifying changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the audit judgment from its own evidence path; deterministic runtime code enforces freshness and blocks completion when the verdict is insufficient. - Claim being audited: the main coding agent&#39;s claim that the current non-trivial implementation is correct and ready to finish. - Ordinary reporting path: the author agent&#39;s normal tool results, implementation narrative and self-checks. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only verifier runs project checks and reads changed files directly under an authoritative scope. - Independence boundary: fresh model-backed subagent, no write tools, no nested subagents, tool-evidence-only instructions and no reliance on the author&#39;s claimed test outcome. - Who acts on findings: the main agent must repair reported failures and invoke a new verification run; the runtime completion gate prevents success until the required PASS is fresh. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/loop/independentVerification.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/verification.md; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/builtinVerificationAgent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic code-review skills are not used for this claim; the positive S3 path is the enforced built-in verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying future/external condition is placed under a function that develops adaptation options and returns them into current S3 capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto-memory, compaction-time consolidation, provider health/recovery, web/browser tools, goals, schedules and plugin/skill configuration can affect later work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation extracts preferences/conventions/lessons/debugging notes and injects retained project knowledge into later sessions; that is persistence/learning context rather than an evidenced outside-and-then option-development conversation with current S3. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/memory/AutoMemoryManager.ts; packages/cli/src/memory/MemoryConsolidation.ts; packages/cli/src/goals/executionFrontier.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has substantial persistence and external tooling, but these do not satisfy the Methodology 0.3.6 external-and-prospective option loop without an adaptation owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is routed to a distinct authoritative S5 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, workspace trust, system prompts, tool policy, plugin/MCP security and configuration constrain execution but do not create identity-governance closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Blade Code agents operate within developer/user-authored constraints and do not have authority to redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/config/PermissionChecker.ts; packages/cli/src/security/WorkspaceTrustService.ts; packages/cli/src/prompts/sections.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator permissions and configuration are legitimate constraints, not identity/ultimate-policy governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96/assessments/blade-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96\">37d2b8f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T23:52:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SICA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent/tree/ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\">ed8275d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party agent loops perform model-directed coding/source inspection, sandboxed shell operations, benchmark problem solving and independently executing delegated workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New bug/task descriptions, mutable repository content, tool errors, model output and local experiment conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects concrete coding steps and allowed tools after receiving tool outputs or prior subagent results. - Decision owner: Executing CodingAgent/ProblemSolver child model, MainOrchestrator&#39;s bounded solver actions and base agent tool-call continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent classes, tool parser and call graph, executecommand, file and edit tools, sandbox Docker process. - Closure path: Task → model-directed operations → actual tool/command outputs and artifact → subsequent step or returned result, with sandbox limits. - Boundary reachability: Packaged baseagent.agent runs MainOrchestratorAgent, which can create coding/model workers; shipped runner.py invokes that executable in isolated Docker containers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-specific coding decisions are model-generated rather than only an external benchmark script. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/coder.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py; baseagent/src/tools/executecommand.py; runner.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent distinct concurrent benchmark-running agent S1 workers from interfering through shared host HTTP ports and shared benchmark result-tracker mutation. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple Python/Docker child coding agents each solve separate benchmark problems through runbenchmarkswithjobqueue and processjob, independently maintaining a coding task loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: If two independently executing benchmark agents bind the same host port 8080/808x, one container fails to start; concurrent writes to the same benchmark JSONL tracker may lose or corrupt result/progress state. - Attenuating coordination relation: runbenchmarkswithjobqueue allocates a unique workerid from bounded idqueue; processjob binds container host port 808{workerid}. Each benchmark uses asyncio.Lock around tracker start/result updates, preventing simultaneous writes to shared provenance. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each worker occupies one reserved ID/port while running its model and returns ID only on exit; subsequent agent work waits for a safe slot or starts on another allocated ID; results can be safely saved and analyzed for next iteration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party ID lease and tracker lock address two named interference modes produced by distinct simultaneously acting model agents; the evidence goes beyond mere task assignment/queue routing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Host port contention and cross-worker result-store writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate/release exclusive host port and serialize benchmark tracker mutations. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic idqueue and per-benchmark asyncio.Lock constructor, not an autonomous S2 mediator model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded maxworkers, unique Docker names, per-problem workdir mounts and benchmark data lock. - Closure path: Several runnable problem jobs → lease workerid/unique host port → parallel model sessions → guarded tracker write → release lease → next job. - Boundary reachability: Default runner.py uses this first-party benchmark job runner during evaluation of each agent iteration; positive S2 is bounded to this concurrent benchmark-agent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safe exclusive-resource scheduling is deterministic host-owned (C), not the meta agent&#39;s discretionary choice. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/benchmarks/base.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven executive direction of multiple current subordinate coding workers with shared task progress and corrective redirection based on their execution results. - Whole-system current view: MainOrchestrator owns the top-level task and receives the complete problem and a synthesized live execution tree from CallGraphManager after subordinate calls, including already attempted work/results and current subordinate execution state. - Current-control decision scope: MainOrchestrator selects/decomposes active subagent tasks, assigns complete subtask instructions, scrutinizes their returned evidence, changes the plan, commissions further workers/verification and decides when a coherent current task result is submitted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagent failures, incomplete work, duplicate approaches, requirement mismatch and uncertain current progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Discretionary model-controlled reassignment, continuation/stop and verification of the current multi-agent goal; no reliance solely on fixed queue. - Decision owner: First-party MainOrchestratorAgent LLM (A) at parent/current multiunit recursion, separate from subordinate child-model operational work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CallGraphManager, generated execution-tree event, model-driven agent calls, structured task results, cost/time governors. - Closure path: Current top-level intent → orchestrator decomposition → model worker runs → returned results plus direct callgraph operating state → revised plans/delegation and final task acceptance. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py constructs MainOrchestratorAgent and starts it as the initial executing actor in CLI and benchmark mode; role documented in the agent&#39;s first-party core prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent model uses evidence and discretionary judgment to govern concurrent/successive independently thinking subagent units, rather than merely running a predefined list of subtasks. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate model-driven runtime monitor checks independent live callgraph/effect events for subagent nonprogress, looping and abnormal execution and can issue corrective notices or terminate faulty child work. - Claim being audited: Operational actors&#39; implicit assertions of continuing useful work and healthy nested execution; notably a child may be silently looping or failing while its parent awaits results. - Ordinary reporting path: Each coding child returns self-authored work and final text to its parent, potentially omitting stalled nested activity. - Complementary access path: An independently launched Overseer model periodically/event-triggeredly reads generateoverseerexecutiontree(CallGraphManager), a first-party event-derived live execution graph, not only the child/parent&#39;s narrative result. This is source-observed execution, although not an independently executed code quality benchmark. - Independence boundary: Overseer is a separate model invocation outside producer and supervising parent tool-delegation turn, with its own model option and timing/event observations. Shared first-party callgraph and provider limits remain; no external independent auditor claim. - Who acts on findings: Overseer itself publishes OVERSEERNOTIFICATION to an implicated agent/parent and calls CallGraphManager.cancelagent when model judge sets forcecancelagent; parent responds via next tool/model decision. It also adapts future monitoring cadence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task nonprogress, recursion loops, stalled/erratic worker execution and hidden operational states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model independently decides concern/notifiability, which actor to notify, whether to cancel, and whether future checks are time/event-driven. - Decision owner: Separate autonomous Overseer model, not a rule-only timeout checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventBus subscriptions, execution-tree renderer, OverseerJudgement schema, LLM check interval, notifications and cancellation path. - Closure path: Real child event and call graph → separate model Overseer diagnosis → notification or direct cancel → parent agent control loop sees issue and can reassign; subsequent oversight resamples. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py always instantiates and starts Overseer when running packaged agent; its monitoringloop subscribes to tool result/assistant events while root agent executes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit&#39;s judgment and direct cancellation/intervention are separate model decisions using a complementary event-derived operating view; not a producer self-review or passive observability dashboard. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/oversight/overseer.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/reporting.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for wiring, medium for audit effectiveness - Caveats: Overseer is an execution-health complementary audit, not independent code correctness review. It may share model provider and callgraph evidence with producer; cancellation can be mistaken or delayed. ReviewCommittee is separately model-driven design critique but is not credited as direct runtime correctness audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven future-capability adaptation: evaluate externally defined benchmark tasks and past agent-version results, generate and implement software architecture/tool changes for subsequent agent generations. - External distinction: Benchmark tasks and measured grader outcomes originate outside the current coding agent&#39;s self-report; the archive records past failures, costs and utility scores. Published literature/web exploration can inform new feature options in the meta-improvement reasoning workflow. - Future / prospective distinction: The meta agent is tasked to improve the next version&#39;s general coding competence, not just solve the currently evaluated benchmark problem or fix the present code task. - Adaptation option generated: MetaImprovementReasoningStructure directs archive analysis (working and failed features), a model-chosen new feature/strategy, code change proposal, multi-member read-only ReviewCommittee critique, model-directed implementation and planned tests. - Path back into current capability / S3: runner.py evaluates a version, selects a base version by stored benchmark statistics and confidence bound, copies code into next iteration, runs that version&#39;s model-driven improve mode on its own codebase, then rebuilds Docker and runs subsequent version benchmarks; resulting changed tool/prompt/coding policy becomes future subordinate S1 capability used by MainOrchestrator&#39;s current-work organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term changes in measured coding benchmark performance and repeated feature failures, rather than current tool-loop transient errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses which agent architecture/tool/behavior feature should be changed after comparing archived experience and review committee feedback, with real downstream implementation and retest. - Decision owner: Meta-improvement MainOrchestrator/CodingAgent and archive analysis model in the explicit self-improvement operating mode; version selection/testing is first-party deterministic support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmark registry, archive analyzer with utility/CI, selectbaseagent, independent model-driven ReviewCommittee, meta-improvement reasoning structure, agentchangelog.md, isolated next-agent directory. - Closure path: External benchmark/task outcomes → version-level archive → model diagnosis and novel future improvement option → separate committee critique → actual coding changes to next version → newly launched/evaluated agent iteration and updated archive. - Boundary reachability: Documented python runner.py default cycle executes benchmarks and then runmetaagentbenchmark, which launches baseagent improve mode. That mode integrates meta-improvement reasoning and committee. No external company service is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Future capability choice and proposal/refinement are model-authored, not merely a static benchmark-triggered template or heuristic version selector; first-party script closes deployment/evaluation. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/tools/reasoningstructures/metaimprovement.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/archiveexplorer.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for model-driven improvement cycle, low for empirical net improvement - Caveats: A new iteration is produced even when benchmark gains are not established; selection uses observed statistics but does not prove monotonic improvement, safety or benchmark transfer. No production performance results inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced legitimate ultimate-purpose/policy authority with identity-level deliberation and binding return across all future agent adaptations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risk of benchmark Goodharting, security tradeoffs, resource spending and deciding which future changes are acceptable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Humans configure objectives/budget, benchmark suite, model credentials and Docker trust. Committee can critique designs but cannot ratify ultimate identity or adjudicate mission change. - Decision owner: External experiment operator and developer-set prompt/policy boundaries; no dedicated legitimate S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewCommittee &#39;taste maker&#39;, testing manager, fixed guards, benchmark score criterion, sandbox/budget limits. - Closure path: Committee reports recommendations and model may refine next feature; no identity-level issue/ultimate authority/legitimate binding policy return at S5 recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A sophisticated self-improvement S4 or design-review committee does not imply governing constitutional/organizational identity authority. - Evidence: baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; runner.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/reviewcommitteemember.py; baseagent/src/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee/assessments/sica.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee\">3efb20a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T22:28:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CocoPlus</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus/tree/b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\">b252a8e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist/persona agents autonomously perform the data-engineering/project transformations defined by the active CocoPlus goal/flow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requirements, code/data artifacts, tool/Snowflake results, tests, implementation uncertainty and bounded project-state changes within each specialist remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local implementation/analysis/tool actions needed to complete the assigned stage/iteration outcome. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoPlus specialist/producer agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: personas, Snowflake Coco host, phase/stage contracts, model-tier floors, isolated worktrees, artifacts and tool/policy hooks. - Closure path: specialists produce artifacts/evidence in their bounded stage; outputs are persisted and passed to later stage/review/current-control paths, affecting subsequent project work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates/hook policy restrict actions but do not choose the specialist&#39;s substantive solution; the model-driven actor owns local operational judgment. - Evidence: README.md and CocoForge/CocoFlow documentation describe specialist agents executing build/test/review/project stages and autonomous long-running modes. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate dependencies and destructive concurrent interference among specialist/producer S1 units while preserving useful parallelism. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-invalid stage starts, concurrent producers touching overlapping file scopes, incomplete work contaminating another agent&#39;s context and oscillation between parallelism levels after failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which approved-plan work can run simultaneously versus must wait, and adapt stage parallelism from current dependency/failure conditions. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoHarvest/Team Lead coordination actor operating under the first-party flow contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph/gates, isolated worktrees, disjoint producer filescope, checkpoint artifacts, concurrency/retry budgets and deterministic dispatch guards. - Closure path: the coordination decision changes which stages/producers dispatch, which wait, and the scope they receive; dependency/checkpoint failures and disjoint-scope enforcement prevent unsafe execution until the coordination condition changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dependency/worktree mechanisms enforce safe boundaries, while the agentic harvest/lead path chooses the useful parallelization/coordination response within them. - Evidence: docs/features.html calls CocoHarvest the intelligence behind parallelism and describes dependency-aware waits/isolated worktrees/adaptive stage parallelism; AGENTS.md.template requires concurrent producer scopes to remain disjoint. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: plain phase sequencing is not credited; the positive witness is the concrete dependency/write-contamination disturbance with an agent-owned parallelism decision. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialist/persona/producer agents executing separate CocoFlow/CocoForge/CocoFleet work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes or dependency-invalid parallel stages can contaminate shared state, create conflicting outputs or cause downstream work to start from incomplete prerequisites. - Attenuating coordination relation: CocoHarvest/Team Lead chooses parallel versus waiting/dependency order; disjoint file scopes, isolated worktrees and checkpoint gates enforce that chosen separation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: only admitted stages/producers dispatch, blocked dependencies remain waiting, and failure/regression signals can narrow parallelism before later execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is explicitly tied to preventing cross-agent contamination and dependency conflict, not merely assigning or ordering tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current whole-run commitments, specialist dispatch, iteration/milestone progression, budgets, recovery and live intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing iterations, budget pressure, stalled stages, specialist output disagreement, quality-gate failure, current run-policy constraints and exceptions requiring developer intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode — choose iteration plan/dispatch/synthesis/next milestone response and current recovery within hard bounds; parent mode — clear human-gated stages, steer/stop/resume or resolve a dispute when current-control authority is deliberately reserved to the developer. - Decision owner: autonomous mode — model-driven Team Lead/run coordinator; parent mode — developer/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge-state.json goal/iteration/phase/budget/team/history, milestone gates, run-policy snapshots, stall detection, retry budgets, AGENTSTOP/STEER, human-gate files and deterministic pre-tool policy hooks. - Closure path: autonomous lead decisions change specialist assignments, iteration state and next-stage progression; parent decisions clear/retain gates, resolve disputes or stop/steer the run, and the persisted state/policy governs subsequent agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Team Lead owns current orchestration within the established run boundary; hard hooks enforce constraints, while explicitly documented human gates/dispute/steering paths preserve a separate parent-owned mode rather than being attributed to the runtime. - Evidence: forge.skill.md assigns plan/dispatch/synthesis/milestone-gate decisions to Team Lead and persists whole-run state; AGENTS.md.template supplies AGENTSTOP/STEER, non-bypassable human gates and run-policy boundaries; docs/features.html supplies recovery/escalation/adaptive stage control. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: static policy enforcement by hooks is not credited as S3 ownership; the mapping depends on Team Lead discretion and explicit returned parent decisions. - Whole-system current view: current forge goal, iteration, phase, budget status, team, history, stage dependencies, evidence/critic status and run-policy snapshot. - Current-control decision scope: iteration plan, specialist dispatch, synthesis, milestone progression, current recovery/parallelism and developer-gated intervention.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge specialist/producer quality claims before milestone/signoff using a structurally separated critic path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-approval, rationalization, hidden quality regressions and acceptance of evidence that does not meet stated criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue PASS/REJECT quality judgment from the independent evidence presented to the critic and gate progression accordingly. - Decision owner: model-driven Quality Critic / read-only fleet critic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: critic read-only role, lack of specialist reasoning traces, default-REJECT stance, producer/critic separation, required PASS signoff and milestone/refinement gates. - Closure path: critic verdict enters the milestone/flow state; rejection prevents acceptance/progression and drives another fix iteration/dispute, while PASS permits the governed continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic agent owns the quality judgment; read-only/separation/gating mechanisms preserve independence and enforce its consequences but do not produce the judgment. - Evidence: AGENTS.md.template states that the Quality Critic receives iteration outputs but not specialist reasoning traces, starts from REJECT and cannot be overruled by Team Lead; fleet mode separates producers from read-only critics and can require PASS; forge.skill.md places critic verdict in each milestone gate. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: routine tests/evidence gates are not the S3 witness; the mapping is specifically the independent critic path with complementary output-level access and non-overridable feedback. - Claim being audited: that specialist/producer iteration output meets the stated goal/quality/evidence criteria and is safe to accept for progression. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist/producer returns its output/artifacts into the Team Lead/flow iteration. - Complementary access path: separate Quality Critic/read-only critic receives iteration outputs/evidence without the specialist reasoning trace and evaluates them under its own criteria/default-REJECT stance. - Independence boundary: critic is separated from producer, read-only in fleet mode, lacks producer reasoning traces and cannot be overruled by Team Lead. - Who acts on findings: Team Lead/flow milestone control consumes PASS/REJECT; rejection forces another iteration, withheld signoff or dispute escalation before progression.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: inspect externally changed model capability after an upgrade, determine how that changes the value of existing governance/harness rules and develop evidence-backed adaptation options for future operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a newer model may make standing rules/checks newly redundant, advisory or still necessary, creating risk of stale harness constraints or unsafe premature deletion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which recurring rule/check should remain load-bearing, degrade to advisory or be proposed as obsolete after model-capability change. - Decision owner: constructor path — CocoPlus supplies the S4-specific model-upgrade hygiene analysis, but the standard path does not autonomously close destructive rule changes; developer composition/authority is still required for returned adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: $hygiene --model-upgrade, evidence collection across sessions/committed artifacts, retrospective/benchmark recommendations and prohibition on silent deletion. - Closure path: hygiene produces evidence-backed keep/advisory/obsolete recommendations intended to change recurring hooks/skills/standing context, but destructive adaptation is proposed rather than automatically applied; downstream developer action must complete the return into present capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can perform the prospective analysis, yet CocoPlus intentionally withholds autonomous closure over the recurring harness rules; this is therefore a supplied S4 constructor path rather than A. - Evidence: cocohygiene/hygiene.skill.md explicitly defines a model-upgrade audit of governance rules/harness checks and classifies their future status while requiring evidence and proposing rather than silently applying deletions; AGENTS.md.template directs retrospective/hygiene/model benchmarking before changing recurring rules. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic retrospectives/learning are not credited as S4. The positive mapping is narrowly the external model-upgrade path; C reflects incomplete first-party adaptation closure. - External distinction: the capability/behavior of the upgraded external model relative to the assumptions under which current CocoPlus rules were created. - Future / prospective distinction: whether recurring governance/harness checks will remain necessary, become merely advisory or become obsolete for subsequent sessions/runs. - Adaptation option generated: retain the rule, downgrade its standing, replace/adjust it or propose removal when evidence shows it is obsolete. - Path back into current capability / S3: the recommendation targets recurring hooks/skills/standing context used by current runs, but developer action is still needed to apply the proposed structural change; this incomplete closure is why the state is C.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy function is established at the reviewed project/run recursion beyond developer-authored policies, covenants and safety boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: autonomy covenants, hard stops, run policies, safety rules and human approvals constrain operation, but the evidence does not show a legitimate S5 identity/ultimate-policy conversation with closure separate from those constraints/current-control gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party S5 decision right is established. - Decision owner: none established for a qualifying S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: covenant acceptance, run-policy snapshots, hard safety gates, human gates, policy engine, steering restrictions and persistent project configuration. - Closure path: these controls constrain subsequent operation, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 judgment → returned organizational policy loop is established at the chosen recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither a static covenant/policy nor human approval over ordinary current work is sufficient to establish S5; no autonomous or parent S5 closure is inferred from enforcement. - Evidence: README.md, AGENTS.md.template, forge.skill.md and pre-tool-use.js were inspected for ultimate-policy/identity authority; the evidenced functions are current governance, safety and developer control. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351/assessments/cocoplus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351\">e7e949d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T15:07:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MegaAgent</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent/tree/c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\">c2e45ad &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded project contribution through a model-driven local task/tool loop and produce shared artifacts, tests, integration or other project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, collaborator messages, current shared artifacts, execution/test results, local TODO/status, tool failures and changing subtask needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local tool actions, artifact changes, communication, testing/execution steps and whether to recruit subordinate capacity within assigned constraints. - Decision owner: each model-driven Agent operational unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent queue/thread, memory and relevant-history retrieval, generated role prompt, bounded model rounds, task/TODO files, first-party tools, shared workspace and external model transport. - Closure path: assigned/local task + collaborator/artifact state → agent model decision → tool/message/file action → returned execution/file/peer observation → subsequent model decision → local deliverable/result. - Boundary reachability: root main.py directly instantiates the generated CEO and workers through the first-party Agent class; recursive addagent uses the same runtime. No downstream agent loop has to be supplied to obtain model-driven operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/tool/runtime code transports and enforces actions, but substantive local work, tool selection, content and follow-up are chosen by each running model agent from current evidence. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, llm.py. - Basis: explicit + structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every generated process is automatically a separate viable recursion; S1 credits task-owning operational agent loops at the declared project level.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate dependency/readiness mismatch and conflicting concurrent mutations among distinct parallel agent S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one unit acting before a required upstream artifact is available; two units observing/writing different versions of a shared artifact; stale overwrites and merge conflicts in the common project workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to adjust local timing/work after collaborator readiness feedback and how to reconcile/retry a shared-artifact update after current content/hash or merge-conflict evidence is returned. - Decision owner: the affected model-driven operational agents, distributed across the collaborating S1 units; the Git lock/base-hash/merge machinery detects and enforces collision constraints but does not choose the revised content/work response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated collaborator identities, asynchronous talk, per-agent TODO/status, shared file visibility, readfile current commit hash, writefile lock/base-hash requirement, Git commit/merge and conflict response. - Closure path: inter-agent dependency or shared-file collision risk → peer readiness message and/or version-aware write attempt → current readiness/content/hash/conflict evidence reaches affected agent → model chooses wait/retry/reconcile/change work → subsequent S1 behavior uses the coordinated state. - Boundary reachability: talk, shared-file read/write and Git conflict handling are standard tools generated for every root-runtime agent; the shipped Grace trace demonstrates the dependency-feedback path without downstream wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a lock alone would be deterministic S2 support only. A is credited because concrete dependency/collision evidence is returned into a model-owned next decision: agents choose to wait, notify collaborators, reread or revise the content/work response. - Evidence: agent.py, llm.py, utils.py, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic messaging/delegation is not the basis; the credited witnesses are concrete dependency timing and shared mutable-artifact collision. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running task-owning agents such as implementation, integration and testing workers. - Inter-S1 disturbance: downstream work can begin before upstream deliverables exist, and parallel agents can act on stale/conflicting versions of shared files. - Attenuating coordination relation: peer readiness/blocker exchange plus hash-aware serialized Git mutation with current-state/conflict feedback. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the receiving model agent waits/resumes/reprioritizes or rereads/reconciles/retries against the returned shared state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: each credited mechanism is tied to a concrete cross-unit instability—dependency readiness or concurrent shared-artifact mutation—and changes the next operational behavior that would otherwise interfere.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the project-wide current portfolio of commitments, priorities, readiness, defects and completion/rework on behalf of the whole organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or blocked workers, changing dependency readiness, integration/testing findings, defects requiring reassignment, project-wide quiescence with unfinished work and apparent completion that may still fail whole-project requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current task allocation/reallocation, testing priorities, corrective owner, additional agent capacity and whether the project should terminate or another work cycle should begin. - Decision owner: the model-driven CEO agent (Bob in the shipped default organization). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker talk reports, CEO subordinate map, shared project artifacts, per-agent TODO/status, global running/idle monitor, automatic wake-up of unfinished agents and final all-idle completion signal. - Closure path: current whole-project reports/artifacts/global completion state → CEO current-control judgment → talk/addagent/inspection/testing/reassignment decision → affected S1 unit changes work → new reports/artifacts/tests return into CEO control. - Boundary reachability: root main.py always creates the generated CEO, sends it the project-start allocation instruction, monitors every live agent/TODO, and returns apparent-completion state to that same CEO for terminate-versus-reassign judgment. The bundled Bob trace shows live priority and corrective routing in this standard path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the global monitor deterministically observes/wakes and supplies state, but it does not choose the organizational response. The CEO model chooses who should work on what next, which defect/priorities matter and whether to create another corrective cycle. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, logs/Bob.log, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial decomposition alone would not establish S3. The positive mapping rests on the later live worker/test feedback and CEO intervention/reassignment loop plus the whole-organization completion/rework closure. - Whole-system current view: worker progress/blocker/test messages, shared output artifacts and the root monitor&#39;s all-agent idle/TODO state provide the CEO with current project-level distinctions rather than a single-child result only. - Current-control decision scope: current ownership, priorities, testing focus, corrective reassignment, additional staffing and terminate-versus-rework decisions affecting multiple operational units and whole-project completion. - Current-control intervention: Bob redirects Dave after Grace identifies an AI defect, instructs Grace to continue/re-test and can recruit/message workers or restart work after the final completion check. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: talk/reassignment enters the relevant agent queue; corrected artifacts and new tester/integrator reports return through the same first-party organization. - Why this is S3-specific rather than decomposition / routing / static workflow: the credited witness occurs after initial decomposition and changes live commitments in response to current cross-project evidence and defects.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary implementation/integration claims by inspecting and executing produced artifacts, then return discrepancies into corrective project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latent functional defects or incomplete behavior that are not revealed by ordinary implementer progress/completion messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from direct artifact/runtime testing whether a produced behavior passes or exposes a concrete defect and publish that finding into project control. - Decision owner: the distinct model-driven testing agent in the generated organization (Grace in the shipped default run); CEO S3 owns the subsequent corrective allocation rather than the tester silently modifying the implementation claim. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tester role assignment, readfile, execpythonfile/interactive execution, test artifacts/reports, talk to CEO/integrator, shared workspace and CEO reassignment channel. - Closure path: implementer/integrator reports and produced artifacts → separate tester reads/executes/tests operational output → tester identifies discrepancy and reports evidence → CEO routes finding to responsible implementation S1 and orders follow-up/re-test → corrected operation is tested/reported again. - Boundary reachability: the standard root organization-generation path can and in the bundled default run does create a distinct tester; its runtime uses the same first-party tools/message channels and the committed logs show the full finding-to-correction handoff. No external evaluator is imported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the testing judgment and finding are produced by a separate model agent using direct operational evidence. Deterministic execution/file tools expose reality but do not decide whether the observed behavior satisfies the claim. - Evidence: main.py, llm.py, logs/Grace.log, logs/Bob.log. - Basis: observed bundled trace + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic logs and the CEO&#39;s own routine project monitoring are not credited as S3. The positive witness is the separately assigned tester&#39;s direct artifact/runtime evidence and its corrective return. - Claim being audited: implementation/integration units report that delivered modules and assembled behavior are ready or functioning. - Ordinary reporting path: implementers/integrator send progress/readiness messages to collaborators/CEO and update shared deliverables/status. - Complementary access path: a distinct tester reads the actual produced files and executes targeted behavior/tests rather than accepting those status reports. - Independence boundary: Grace is separately generated/assigned as testing work, not the producer of the AI/integration implementation being challenged; her test observations are reported to the CEO rather than being generated by Dave&#39;s own production loop. - Who acts on findings: Grace reports the hard-AI four-in-a-row failure to Bob; Bob sends the finding to Dave for defensive-logic correction, directs Grace to continue testing, and the later organization requests re-testing/integration feedback. - Why this is S3-specific rather than ordinary review / QA / logging / evaluation: the evidence path materially bypasses ordinary implementer claims, directly probes runtime behavior, finds a contradiction and closes that finding into corrective operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop that changes future MegaAgent organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime reacts strongly to current task complexity, current shared artifacts and current test outcomes, but no evidenced function models changing external/future conditions for later organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and promoting a future capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamic agent generation, recursive addagent, memory/history retrieval, current-task testing and task-specific reorganization. - Closure path: current task/complexity → current roster/subtask adaptation → current project execution; no prospective environmental distinction → future adaptation options → selected/promoted later capability → present S3 conversation is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents autonomously change current staffing and work in response to present operational variety, but Profile 0.2.3 explicitly separates current task planning/reaction from S4 future/environment intelligence. - Evidence: README.md, agent.py, config.py. - Basis: structural absence after prospective-adaptation review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dynamically generating hundreds of agents demonstrates current variety amplification/scaling, not by itself future-oriented S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the MegaAgent project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the user project goal, CEO name, model/provider choice, maximum rounds/subordinates and additional prompt constrain current operation, but no identity-level S3–S4 tension or ultimate-policy issue enters an authoritative runtime closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config.initialprompt, additionalprompt, user-edited project goal/provider settings, static limits and CEO role generation. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the CEO is an executive/control role for the current project, but executive naming and ordinary project completion authority are not S5. The user supplies initial purpose/configuration before operation; no parent runtime identity escalation path is shown. - Evidence: config.py, main.py, README.md. - Basis: structural absence after identity/policy review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could wrap MegaAgent in human/institutional governance, but that would be a different system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11/assessments/megaagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11\">2022cc4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAGS</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher/tree/9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\">9bb9dfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist research agents perform the operational scientific work: literature review, proposal development, experiments, manuscript production, and peer review/revision artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended scientific questions, external literature, experiment implementation/execution, result interpretation, and manuscript construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next specialist reasoning/tool/file action needed to produce each role&#39;s research outcome within its SOUL/skill contract. - Decision owner: each first-party builtin OpenAGS Agent executing the corresponding specialist folder/role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL and skill loading, tool engine, memory/session/history, filesystem workspaces, upstream/downstream paths, LiteLLM transport, timeouts, and orchestrator lifecycle. - Closure path: observations/tool results return to the specialist Agent loop; the agent updates role artifacts/status and those outputs become available to downstream agents/coordinator. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents a complete first-party builtin Agent path (Agent.loop) used by the research layer; S1 therefore does not depend on Claude Code/Codex/Cursor/Gemini owning the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party agent engine closes model decision → tool execution → observation → next decision inside the supported builtin mode. - Evidence: builtin-agent execution path, folder-as-agent model, default specialist workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: alternate CLI-agent backends are not used to justify S1 ownership; the credited mode is the self-contained builtin OpenAGS agent engine.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated inter-S1 conflict/oscillation is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: upstream/downstream file contracts, fixed first-pass sequence, TASKS/STATUS files, agent dispatch, and shared project artifacts organize work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 interference witness. - Closure path: workflow outputs feed downstream specialists, but that sequencing/dependency path is not sufficient for S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: named multi-agent roles and communication through files are generic organization/hand-off primitives unless tied to a concrete conflict or oscillation between S1 units and an attenuation loop. - Evidence: file-based agent communication, coordinator first-pass sequence. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: workflow dependency management may prevent some practical clashes, but the required S2-specific disturbance/attenuation witness is not established in the reviewed evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current view and decide which specialist work starts, waits, reruns, or is complete. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed stages, changing project status, downstream dependency readiness, review-identified weaknesses, and need to redirect current research effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next active specialist/task and, after the first pass, choose which research stages must rerun in response to current project evidence. - Decision owner: the root coordinator Agent in Auto Mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist STATUS.md and TASKS.md, workflow/orchestrator process management, timeout/failure handling, fixed initial stage order, project files, and start/wait/all-complete action protocol. - Closure path: coordinator reads current statuses/review findings, emits startagent/wait/allcomplete/needshuman decisions and writes/assigns specialist tasks; the orchestrator launches or withholds subsequent work accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Auto Mode and the root coordinator are part of the default first-party project template and builtin workflow, not an external operator or development-only controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic orchestration executes start/stop/status mechanics, while the coordinator Agent is explicitly instructed to make pipeline decisions and decide what happens next from the whole project state. - Whole-system current view: all specialist status files, project-level memory/key decisions, current stage completion, and review outputs across the research project. - Current-control decision scope: assignment and ordering of current specialist work, whether to wait, whether the project is complete, and which stages to rerun after review. - Evidence: coordinator Auto Mode and role, workflow protocol. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fixed first-pass sequencing is enforcement/support; S3 credit comes from the coordinator&#39;s evidenced discretionary current-control decisions, especially status-based dispatch and post-review rerun choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the manuscript/research claims using complementary access to manuscript, experiment results, literature notes, and external citation verification, then return actionable findings into project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, weak methodology/experiments, citation hallucination or misrepresentation, missing baselines/limitations, and paper-level weaknesses not caught by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make peer-review judgments, classify major/minor concerns, score review criteria, issue a verdict, and prescribe a revision roadmap. - Decision owner: the distinct review Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated review SOUL/folder, read access to manuscript/experiment/literature artifacts, web search for citation spot-checking, structured review format, and persisted review-report.md. - Closure path: review findings are written under review/reviews/; after the first pass the coordinator reads those weaknesses and decides which stages need to rerun, returning audit findings into changed subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: the reviewer is a default specialist in the standard project template and a standard first-pass stage; its report is explicitly part of the coordinator&#39;s iteration mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer makes substantive adversarial/audit judgments rather than merely applying a deterministic syntax gate; the coordinator, not the reviewer, owns corrective project-control decisions. - Claim being audited: the manuscript&#39;s scientific claims, citations, experimental support, logic, completeness, and reproducibility. - Ordinary reporting path: manuscript output and specialist status/results flow normally into the project filesystem and coordinator. - Complementary access path: the reviewer reads the full manuscript plus experiment report plus literature review and independently web-searches sampled citations; it is instructed to adversarially probe alternative explanations/failure conditions/missing experiments. - Independence boundary: a separate review role/folder with its own SOUL and artifact is outside the manuscript-producing agent&#39;s ordinary production loop, while retaining direct access to underlying experiment/literature evidence. - Who acts on findings: the root coordinator reads review weaknesses and selects which stages to rerun; affected specialists then perform the corrective work. - Evidence: reviewer SOUL, coordinator iteration mode. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider independence is not claimed; organizational independence comes from the distinct review role, separate artifact, complementary evidence surfaces, and explicit findings-to-control return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense the external scientific landscape through the literature specialist and turn those distinctions into future-facing research proposals/experiment direction consumed by later S1 work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk of pursuing already-solved, poorly grounded, or scientifically irrelevant directions and missing externally established methods/results that should shape the project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and synthesize externally retrieved papers/field distinctions that should inform the project proposal and later research program. - Decision owner: the literature Agent, with downstream proposal/experiment agents autonomously using the returned research intelligence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-search skills/web access, literature notes/memory, fixed upstream paths, shared project filesystem, and coordinator sequencing. - Closure path: literature agent searches/reads external work and writes literature notes; the proposal agent explicitly reads ../literature/notes/ and memory.md; experiment/manuscript stages consume the proposal/literature outputs, changing subsequent project work. - Boundary reachability: literature is a default first-party specialist stage in the project template, and its artifacts are wired as upstream inputs to subsequent specialists in every supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party literature Agent decides what external evidence is relevant and synthesizes it; file plumbing only returns that intelligence to current capability. - External distinction: current prior papers, methods, results, and gaps retrieved from outside the project. - Future / prospective distinction: which field gaps/constraints should shape the proposal and experiments the project has not yet executed. - Adaptation option generated: literature-grounded research/proposal directions and constraints for experiment design. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted literature notes become explicit upstream inputs to proposal and later experiment/manuscript work under coordinator sequencing. - Evidence: folder/agent upstream-downstream architecture, research workflow skill. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project memory and peer review alone are not used to justify S4; the credit rests on external scientific sensing and a closed return path into future project work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the research-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL role definitions, user project brief, templates, skills, provider configuration, and needshuman escalation constrain operation but do not themselves establish ultimate-policy governance. - Closure path: no evidenced identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator manages current work but is not shown redefining the project&#39;s ultimate identity/policy; generic human input/escalation is not automatically parent-governed S5. - Evidence: coordinator SOUL, architecture. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can direct a project and the coordinator can request human input, but neither fact alone establishes S5 without a genuine ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/openags.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T20:00:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCursor</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor/tree/b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\">b4528f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly interpret, inspect, edit and verify software against a human coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code and runtime behavior, incomplete edits, tool errors and feedback from repository/test inspection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose file reads, edits, tool calls, retries, delegated S1 work and final response based on live evidence. - Decision owner: the model-driven coding agent in Agent mode and coding-worker subagents in supported Project/Multitask modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: VS Code tool bridge, file and shell effect adapters, permission/approval policies, session persistence and job display. - Closure path: agent choice → tool execution/permission result → actual file/shell observation → subsequent model decision or answer. - Boundary reachability: the packaged extension directly wires its agent loop and repository tools, no custom embedding host or optional external agent workflow is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the acting model leaves tool wrappers but no task-driven selection/repair; human approves permitted effects but does not choose every next coding action. - Evidence: README.md; loop.ts; index.ts; files.ts; approvalPolicy.ts - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-workspace change collisions and conflicting edits between independently acting delegated code-writing workers in the shipped coordinator/team modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: at least two concurrently active coding children may attempt incompatible modifications or overwrite/revert another worker&#39;s output in their shared checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model selects nonoverlapping assignments, sends corrective messages when a child flags conflict, and revises affected children rather than blindly accepting conflicting edits. - Decision owner: the lead agent/model in opt-in Project/Multitask configurations; child agents report overlaps; deterministic mailbox and a concurrency cap only execute the decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task schema&#39;s &#39;assign separate files&#39; contract, built-in team protocol&#39;s non-overwrite/flag instruction, stable child IDs, collaborator status, message/follow-up controls. - Closure path: child conflict signal or current roster/result → lead&#39;s scope/plan correction → SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent or new separate assignment → children&#39;s subsequent work; separation also happens prospectively when lead initially assigns distinct files. - Boundary reachability: built-in Project/Multitask prompt paths and child/messaging tools are wired in the standard extension, not derived from a user-created orchestration script. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-scope choice and conflict response are discretionary lead-model decisions; the active-child cap and status store do not own that interference-specific decision. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; teams.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; collaborationState.ts - Basis: explicit mode instructions and structural code-wired feedback - Confidence: medium; a supported decision path, not proof that every conflict is detected or prevented - Caveats: children usually share a workspace; conversation worktrees are optional isolation and not automatic per-child reservations. Ordinary Agent mode does not establish this coordinated path on every turn. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model/tool-running coding children started via the Task tool, each with its own history, task and executable edit tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overwriting/reverting another child&#39;s files or incompatible edits in the shared workspace, explicitly addressed by the shipped Team protocol and Task tool. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead chooses separate file assignments; built-in team members must adapt/flag cross-member conflicts, while root can steer/interrupt/reassign them. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child reports and roster responses enter the lead turn; message/follow-up deliver revised instructions to named still-running or resumed children. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped instructions target a concrete shared-file interference, not only transporting tasks; lead feedback can alter worker scopes after conflict.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the set of active delegated commitments across current Project/Multitask coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple live coding children compete for bounded concurrent slots, priorities and dependencies while task results and interruptions change the feasible current plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the complete conversation collaborator roster/status and choose which current worker gets each unit, is steered, followed up or interrupted; revise running commitments in response to results. - Decision owner: the opt-in Project/Multitask lead model; not the fixed max-four gate, job registry, event stream or human authorization engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents and stable IDs, activeAgents map, child status/result feedback, SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent/InterruptAgent, root&#39;s intentionally disabled editing tools. - Closure path: whole-session roster/task reports → lead&#39;s portfolio-level assignment/intervention decision → launch/message/interrupt and changed child work → updated statuses/results. - Boundary reachability: the standard Project and Multitask modes explicitly put an LLM lead over all delegated coding tasks with model-facing operational management tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model leaves a cap and worker status API but not a discretionary choice of how to reallocate current work; the supported lead modes are wider than one routine child tool call. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; collaborationState.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; taskState.ts - Basis: structural and explicit first-party coordinator-mode instructions - Confidence: medium; session-scoped, not organizational or multi-deployment management - Caveats: not attributed to ordinary single-worker Agent mode or separate VS Code sessions. Does not mean that all child steps are autonomously centralized. - Whole-system current view: ListAgents exposes all retained conversation collaborators, live status/result and queued-message counts; background job registry tracks running/completed work. - Current-control decision scope: lead-mode model decides present unit assignment, bounded concurrency slot utilization, correction/interrupt, follow-up and completion of delegated commitments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect the implementing agent&#39;s code claims using a separate read-only code-reviewer context and raw repository/diff evidence, then return findings for control of repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s self-report or ordinary integrated tool-status check may omit real defects, concurrency problems or unsafe edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate reviewer agent evaluates the actual diff and source context for defects; the implementing parent receives and decides follow-up repair or rejection. - Decision owner: the delegated reviewer model for the complementary judgment, acting only when commissioned, and the lead/implementer model for the downstream corrective choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in read-only code-reviewer and team-code-reviewer roles, child independent history, file-reading/search tools, returned report and parent Task result. - Closure path: parent requests review → separate child reads current repository/diff and forms defect-first findings → report returns to parent history → implementing lead can fix, stop or ask for further check. - Boundary reachability: built-in code-reviewer Task type and built-in team-code-reviewer are available in the shipped extension and run through first-party child-model/tool execution; no user-authored reviewer is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer performs an independent direct-evidence judgment; parent runtime reports/forwards but does not supply the reviewer&#39;s verdict. Ordinary VerificationLedger is excluded from S3 credit. - Evidence: teams.ts; schemas.ts; loop.ts; files.ts; verification.ts - Basis: explicit built-in independent reviewer instructions and source-wired child execution - Confidence: medium-high for opt-in separate-context code review, not for default every-turn checks - Caveats: review and corrective response are conditional on the model/parent invoking the supported reviewer; no claim of Organization S3 governance or fully independent remote operator. - Claim being audited: whether implementing-agent code modifications are correct, safe and maintainable. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer tool outputs, final answer and VerificationLedger test-status summary. - Complementary access path: read-only separately instantiated reviewer model with direct code/diff/repository access and explicit defect-first prompt. - Independence boundary: different child model context/history/role and direct artifacts rather than merely parsing the implementer&#39;s natural-language self-report. - Who acts on findings: calling lead/implementer receives findings through Task result and can select corrective work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external-and-prospective organizational capability-adaptation loop is established in the distributed extension. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation, model/tool changes and potential future feature requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S4-specific adaptive decision/feedback owner identified. - Decision owner: model for present coding and documentation retrieval; user or extension settings for selecting models/skills; neither closes future adaptation of the harness organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs discovery/indexing, semantic repository retrieval, reusable personas/instructions, task memory and provider choice. - Closure path: information returns to task context, but no first-party future capability strategy is selected and returned to ongoing operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: document discovery and planning for current coding work is not a prospective S4 organizational judgment. - Evidence: docsPlanner.ts; loop.ts; README.md; taskState.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy constitutional decision path with binding return is supplied for the coding-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose or policy conflicts as distinct from one tool&#39;s allowed/denied action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 decisive authority; ordinary permission review authorizes individual actions. - Decision owner: user/host settings and project operators outside the assessed system-in-focus; no evidenced internal/parent constitutional decision chain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, read-only modes, tool restrictions, workspace instructions and team role prompts. - Closure path: action approvals return allow/deny to ordinary task execution; no identity-level matter → parent ultimate decision → changed governing operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool policy is a safety constraint, not the identity-policy function; an operator&#39;s wider development organization is not imported into this one-session boundary. - Evidence: approvalPolicy.ts; loop.ts; schemas.ts; README.md; teams.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea/assessments/opencursor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea\">37c1aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T12:59:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">orch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent/tree/533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\">533d06b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Generate and repair code in response to scoped user specification and feedback from tests and model calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar source, incomplete interfaces, implementation errors, rejected model outputs and failing frozen tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a task solution/patch from specification, context and feedback, including changed implementation on failure. - Decision owner: Model-backed planner and builder calls in the installed run; deterministic code enforces schema, attempts and test acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktrees, contextpack, prompt budgets, frozen tests, provider adaptors, retry ladder. - Closure path: Model plan → task/test contracts → model patch → sandbox test → accept or issue failure feedback → reattempt/replan/park. - Boundary reachability: plan and execute are first-party orchestrator operations reachable through supported CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The planner and code model choose substantive task decomposition and edits; the scheduler does not author those solutions. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L715-L885, orchrun.py#L3247-L3477, orchrun.py#L3848-L3905. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for a reachable source-level loop; no provider-run success reproduced. - Caveats: The normal run requires configured model inference and an executable project environment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: Parallel independently model-backed task builders, each operating in its own task-specific worktree against the same shared integration branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A builder&#39;s code or contract can overlap another builder&#39;s changed files, produce a Git conflict, or invalidate a frozen test already passed by another task. - Attenuating coordination relation: Planned file ownership/dependencies, serialized merge lock, affected-tests integration gate and explicit capped rebasing of collided tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflict or affected-test failure returns the named task to READY/rebase or retry with updated branch state rather than silently accepting both edits. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The implemented merge-collision-to-task-retry and cross-task test-interference gate regulate actual interaction failures between distinct coding units. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel code-building units in a shared integration branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: File ownership overlap, incompatible dependency interfaces, parallel Git conflicts and tests broken by another worker&#39;s merge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Coordinate dependency/ownership and merge order, reject a conflicting commit and route the affected task back for a refreshed build. - Decision owner: Deterministic DAG scheduler, interface and file gates and integration merge lock; no separate autonomous coordination actor owns the arbitration policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planned dependency edges, serialized integration, affected-test gate, rebase count and changed-worktree tests. - Closure path: Concurrent task outputs → merge collision/affected tests → reject/rebase/retry of specific task → next task execution on updated integration state. - Boundary reachability: execute, ready, merge are standard paths for concurrent task runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision/feedback witness is function-specific and shipped, but the coordinator&#39;s decisive scheduling/merge arbitration is hard-coded; agent-owned S2 would need composition. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2257, orchrun.py#L2420-L2475, orchrun.py#L3848-L3910. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Mere DAG edges would not establish S2 without the identified collision-response/return path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Whole-system current view: The run&#39;s persisted task table, DONE/READY/RUNNING/PARKED statuses, worker occupancy, rate/spend counters and outstanding dependency graph. - Current-control decision scope: Adjust current concurrency and admissions, reorder ready work by dependency criticality, or stop a module with repeated parked tasks; all are currently code-controlled rather than model-owned decisions. - Function: Control the complete current spec-run portfolio&#39;s admissions, throughput, resource limits and stalled tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider rate-limit pressure, too many active model calls, spend caps, blocked dependencies and repeating module failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change current concurrency, dispatch priority and task admissions or park a failed module based on observed whole-run state. - Decision owner: Deterministic scheduler (drain, room, throttle, breaker); models generate tasks but do not autonomously decide whole-system resource intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AIMD limit, token/cache policies, dependency depth sorting, persistent state, failure counts. - Closure path: Ready/in-flight state and failure signals → scheduler resource/priority intervention → changed dispatch and retry conditions for multiple tasks. - Boundary reachability: Standard execution uses drain and applies current maxjobs/maxverifyjobs, rate and spend gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A whole-run feedback and resource-decision path exists, but its discretionary control policy is fixed in code, leaving agent ownership uncomposed. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2294, orchrun.py#L2451-L2490. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; no autonomous allocator or qualified human parent mode evidenced. - Caveats: Ordinary user-set quotas do not independently establish P parent governance.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: The planner&#39;s frozen test contract correctly expresses the original user requirements, notwithstanding a builder&#39;s failing implementation or allegation of wrong tests. - Ordinary reporting path: The builder&#39;s candidate code and sandbox failure result are reported to the retry ladder and orchestration state. - Complementary access path: A separately prompted Claude reviewer receives original specification, requirement mapping, frozen test sources and explicit dispute, not just the builder&#39;s success report. - Independence boundary: Separate persistent review ID, pinned input hash and validated testscorrect/testswrong verdict; the builder cannot directly rewrite frozen tests. - Who acts on findings: review closes a correct-test dispute or fixtests commits guarded test corrections and re-verifies affected candidate and prior merges. - Function: Independently challenge the frozen test contract against the original user specification when task failures indicate a test dispute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong or over-constraining tests drafted before implementation, misleading failure attribution and builder attempts to relax constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An exception-review model independently returns testscorrect or testswrong, with an explanation and guarded test edits; a wrong-test verdict can authorize corrective test revision. - Decision owner: Separate Claude review invocation using original specification, protected from the builder&#39;s own test-edit decision; deterministic code validates the verdict and safe application. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable review inputs, durable review store, assertion-count no-weakening gate and regression check of already merged tasks. - Closure path: Failed worker/test dispute → independent review → validated finding → corrected frozen tests or rejection → saved candidate reverified / task retried. - Boundary reachability: review and fixtests are first-party retry-ladder operations; their execution is conditional, not an asserted always-on audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer has the substantive adjudication right; the builder cannot directly alter the pre-frozen test contract, while code checks and transports the verdict. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L3010-L3140, orchreview.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the dispute-triggered complementary path. - Caveats: Independence is functional/prompt-and-role separation, not a demonstrated separate provider organization; ordinary final pytest gates alone would not establish A.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: Past external project specifications, sandbox/test outcomes and model-builder failures vary between runs; rule candidates are grounded in these encountered environmental distinctions, not only an internal heartbeat. - Future / prospective distinction: The A/B and replay checks compare candidate rules against distinct past specifications to select instructions expected to improve subsequent planning/building runs. - Adaptation option generated: Model-authored, cross-task reusable planner or builder lessons become candidate rules, tested with/without them and potentially activated at later checkpoints. - Path back into current capability / S3: Promoted rules enter the planner or builder prompt layer in a subsequent run, changing the model&#39;s future action repertoire, while harmful rules are retired. - Function: Learn prospective planning/building-rule changes from accumulated cross-run failures and outcomes, and return successful changes into future model prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeating planning defects, weak tests, recurring builder mistakes and the possibility that a proxy for eventual success becomes misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model review/replanning/build attempts formulate generalizable lessons; A/B evidence evaluates whether a candidate helps, with checkpoint promotion and subsequent prompt influence. - Decision owner: Autonomous models propose the adaptive rule content from task observations; deterministic promotion statistics retain activation veto, so the agent does not own final promotion discretion in isolation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rule store, paired trials and replay, proxy-vs-real Spearman trust test, lifecycle candidate → active → retired and prompt layer budget. - Closure path: Outcomes and model lessons from earlier runs → candidate rule → with/without evaluation → promote/retire at checkpoint → altered next-plan/builder instruction. - Boundary reachability: learn, evolve, settleplannerrules and builder rule trials are first-party modes (automatic rule learning disabled in frozen-policy modes). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous models originate strategic adaptation content, not merely execute a preselected fixed retry; promotion is a statistical safeguard rather than a model-authored option. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L1570-L1775, orchrun.py#L3888-L3925, orchrules.py#L1-L125. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium for prospectively reusable operating-policy learning; causal effectiveness not independently reproduced. - Caveats: Feedback comes chiefly from past run/task outcomes, not market sensing. If an evaluator requires a wholly independent outside-environment sensor rather than cross-run experience, S4 should be revisited; the observed loop is durable future capability change, not just current repair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-system identity or ultimate-policy dispute and legitimate-authority closure is evidenced for a spec-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe workspace edits, mismatched model routes, insufficient budgets and altered test contracts are addressed as operational guardrails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Bind an ultimate policy/identity judgment to later operations; no such accountable agent or legitimate parent governance loop is shipped. - Decision owner: User chooses configuration and optional frozen policy; model reviews resolve tests and task strategy, not organizational identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy store, sandbox boundary, explicit model settings, pinned planning inputs, cancel/park limits and review gate. - Closure path: Configuration and safety gates constrain a run; no S5-level issue/appeal → authoritative decision → returned binding identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Guarded model choices and an immutable execution policy are not themselves an S5 constitutional decision. - Evidence: orchpolicy.py, orchrun.py#L715-L815. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer policy and a human&#39;s optional local run selection are outside this selected recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3/assessments/or/orchestragent-orch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3\">987160f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:25:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork/tree/e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\">e97d75c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Claude sessions in a workflow transform requests into code, specifications, reviews and other local project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, repository state, test failures, tool results and child reports within each session&#39;s bounded task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete implementation/review actions and revise work from the tool, branch and report feedback. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude agent node in the first-party runtime; an operator supplies the initial task and permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph activation, SDK/CLI driver, tool allowlists, worktrees, event store, session budget and schema validation. - Closure path: agent chooses tools and edits, receives tool/result feedback, changes work and returns a structured report to its graph/leader. - Boundary reachability: claude.worker/claude.leader nodes and their tool/report bridge are supported installed node types, exercised structurally by shipped example workflows; no third-party agent&#39;s hidden control architecture is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: actions in a live session remain discretionary even though workflow graph edges, budgets and output transport are deterministic. - Evidence: scheduler built-ins and delegation, delegation port, factory example. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider execution was not performed in this review; operator credentials and the chosen workflow are prerequisites.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate competing write access among parallel S1 sessions in the same run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two concurrent writing agents can attempt overlapping files/folders or shared named resources, introducing overwrites or merge conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agents select resources to claim, release them and respond to a refused claim by choosing another task or negotiating with the holder; the registry deterministically detects overlap. - Decision owner: agent session for claim selection and the corrective work choice; deterministic claim registry for overlap verdict, holder identity, event delivery and automatic release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: advisory claim registry keyed by normalized paths, refusal and release notices, worktree isolation and message channels. - Closure path: a conflicting claim is refused with holder identity; the agent receives that response, can communicate/replan, and may retry after a release notification, changing the subsequent S1 work target. - Boundary reachability: claim/release tools are supplied to writing Claude nodes when a run exposes parallel writers; the scheduler binds the run-scoped claim port. This is a shipped operating mode, not a hypothetical custom integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ork does not choose the agent&#39;s alternative work in response to contention; its registry only determines the fixed overlap rule. Claim discipline is advisory, so not every actual edit is protected. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running writer leader/worker nodes or spawned worker copies with separate tasks and checkouts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping resource/path edits and resultant destructive interference, specifically documented as the reason for claims. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit per-resource claim refusal, release, holder/contender notification and optional peer messaging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the refused agent sees the holding node and can work elsewhere or ask it; release notice tells the agent to claim again before editing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: resource overlap is checked in executable first-party code, with a conflict-specific refusal/release feedback loop; message passing is only a support channel. - Evidence: claim registry implementation, Claude claim tool, claims and message specification. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: autonomous conflict handling is agent-instructed but not hard-enforced and was not exercised with a provider here; if the claimed function is defined solely as lock acquisition, that deterministic subdecision is not agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: control a multi-child run&#39;s current commitments and exceptions at a configured leader/whole-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child failure, run budget/concurrency saturation, exhausted revision loops, and conflicting current delivery commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the leader can choose whether and where to delegate/retry in light of child reports; an owner can answer an exhausted-loop exception, while configuration author sets the initial hard limits. - Decision owner: first-party leader/owner decision ports are available; workflow author must compose a genuinely whole-run supervisory objective and authority envelope. The deterministic runner owns enforcement, not the resource-policy judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: slot/budget accounting, maxWorkers cap, statuses, reports, loop counter, human fallback and halt semantics. - Closure path: delegated children return outcomes and costs; the leader can select follow-on work, or answer an exhausted loop to stop/extend/go onward; the scheduler executes the selected transition. - Boundary reachability: leader tools, child reports and owner-question/answer loop are shipped in the scheduler and Claude runtime; their S3-specific whole-run composition depends on the declared workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are real current-intervention primitives, but a generic leader&#39;s task division alone does not establish a default autonomous whole-run S3 owner for arbitrary user workflows. This supports the narrower constructor C, not universal A. - Whole-system current view: available child reports, run status, costs and configured limits; whether they form an actionable whole-system view is workflow-dependent. - Current-control decision scope: child commitments and retry/loop continuation under configured budgets; no assertion that the agent can revise operator-specified global policy. - Evidence: leader/child delegation, loop owner decision, scheduler budget. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no parent-mode suffix from ordinary human gates, pause/stop or local approval; no runtime end-to-end current-control trace tested.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: inspect implementation claims through separately tasked agents that can directly read the produced branch rather than rely only on the builder&#39;s report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects in the builder&#39;s asserted completion, including security, correctness, UX and code-quality issues overlooked by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent readonly reviewer sessions judge the produced work and return pass/changesrequested; the builder then receives review status and revises in a bounded loop. - Decision owner: separate reviewer Claude agents in the first-party software-factory opt-in mode; graph routing enforces returned verdicts, but is not the audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: readonly checkout, fresh reviewer sessions, join statuses, bounded edge maxRounds, agent approver and optional human publication gate. - Closure path: builder branch is inspected by separately running security/UX/code reviewers; a changesrequested result routes back to builder and triggers repair; passing reviews route to the approver. - Boundary reachability: examples/software-factory.ork.yaml is a distributed executable Ork workflow invoking the installed reviewer node types with readonly project access. The path is an opt-in mode, not assumed present in every workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer models decide the audit verdict based on direct artifact access; deterministic joins merely transport outcomes. Their separation is context/process isolation, not institutional independence from the operator or model supplier. - Claim being audited: builder&#39;s assertion that a branch implements the spec correctly and safely. - Ordinary reporting path: builder&#39;s status, summary and artifact/branch report to the workflow. - Complementary access path: security/UX/code agents in readonly workspaces inspect the actual branch and surrounding code, not just the builder&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent sessions, reviewer roles and readonly source access; all are authored within one user-defined workflow, so a malicious author can weaken the separation. - Who acts on findings: builder corrects the branch when any review requests changes; approver examines the passing result before the user-controlled publication step. - Evidence: factory workflow, PR-review workflow, worktree/delegation behavior. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: source-grounded installed example path, not independently executed S3 evaluation or a universal independent-audit service; routine sequential QA in an arbitrary workflow would not automatically qualify.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally oriented prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external demands or future capabilities could require adaptation, but no shipped owner closes that function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an Ork-run S4. - Decision owner: none demonstrated; workflow authors or maintainers may revise YAML and Ork code outside the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured feedback, event store, local feedback list/note/dismiss, context compiler and user-authored scripts. - Closure path: no shipped feedback-to-accepted-change/lesson path into future current run capability at this revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: collection and grouping of past errors are not themselves a future-oriented model/options/return loop. - Evidence: feedback spec with explicit partial implementation, feedback guide, roadmap. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user may build a separate adaptation workflow; that is not shipped S4 closure in the declared default system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path for a workflow organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity or policy tensions can arise, but Ork&#39;s gates and constraints concern routine task/runtime permissions and delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S5-specific issue, legitimate authority judgment and return-to-operation path. - Decision owner: workflow author/operator defines configuration and controls ordinary approval; those actions are not an evidenced identity-policy process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML settings, tool allowlists, budget caps, askuser, human gates, CLI run controls and local auth. - Closure path: ordinary approvals/stop and permission decisions affect run progression, not an established ultimate-policy matter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user or agent can edit instruction text or choose publication, but no system-specific S5 right is constructed or closed. - Evidence: workflow format, message and decision controls, factory human gate. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external human/project may have its own legitimate S5; Ork&#39;s default standard distribution does not supply that governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de/assessments/or/ork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de\">06adb32 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:43:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Synapse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse/tree/b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\">b913457 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous local production and checking of a specific delegated project deliverable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous requirements, codebase/tool errors, rendered defects and incomplete subtasks encountered during task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Role model chooses its own next implementation/test tool after observing results. - Decision owner: Role-specific model agent, reached through Synapse-created engine session; human owns separate scope/permission gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode adapter, per-role tool rules, session brief, tool transcript, provider retries, bounded worker runner and written-artifact ledger. - Closure path: Model tool call→real engine execution→tool feedback→continuing role turn→worker report/files, then return to Architect. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Multi mode makes genuine model-backed role sessions rather than invoking a development mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The role&#39;s model has task-sensitive tool/repair choice; Synapse supplies the enclosing execution and evidence channel. - Evidence: OpenCode adapter, runs.js, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high on reachable code path; no independent Windows-provider execution. - Caveats: Actual model quality, installed/packaged engine availability and end-to-end completion were not live-tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Feedback-based regulation of interference or oscillation among operational S1 task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits, shared project plan corruption and worker cross-role overwrite risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No specific agent-owned inter-worker collision detection→mediation→returned adjustment evidenced. - Decision owner: Deterministic harness serialization and folder rules; Architect decides task sequence as S3 commitment, not evidenced as S2 conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-active-worker busy guard per project, roleWriteRules, plan ownership, role folders and assignment ladder. - Closure path: Serial admission and static write rules prevent some conflicts but do not inspect actual inter-unit interference and negotiate mutual change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, isolation and top-down task allocation alone are insufficient for S2 at this recursion. - Evidence: runs.js#L546, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the reviewed first-party orchestration paths. - Caveats: A model could discuss competing tasks in an individual session, but no reusable S2-specific interference closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Architect reads the project-wide plan.json task statuses, blocked items, confirmed features and per-worker completion reports grounded in runs.jsonl and actual file/tool records. - Current-control decision scope: Architect alone accepts/rejects task completion, alters the whole plan and selects corrective work and next role commitments within approved project scope; a runner&#39;s retry/backoff merely enforces constraints. - Function: Current project-wide commitment, progress and corrective task regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Partial deliverables, faulty worker claims, blocked tasks, failed providers, scope drift and new defects threatening the approved project goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge each reported worker outcome, update the plan, mark done/retry/blocked, reprioritize or delegate the next current task. - Decision owner: Model-driven Architect in Multi mode, constrained by operator-approved features and optional automatic continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan.json as Architect-only authority, actual runs.jsonl, injected post-worker report, task-role assignment, retry fallback, progress/status rail. - Closure path: Worker run evidence→synthetic report→Architect evaluation/update of plan→next delegate or required operator escalation; Keep building enables continuation, while off returns after each judged task. - Boundary reachability: delegate MCP, run ledger, handleTurn, report and wake are connected in shipped Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the Architect leaves recorded runs and deterministic stops but not discretionary whole-project acceptance, replanning or corrective commitment. - Evidence: runs.js#L2741, plans.js#L1270, delegate MCP. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high; project-control closure is code-visible, not a live success claim. - Caveats: An operator can pause or steer a task, but the documented toggles do not independently establish a distinct parent-governed S3 decision mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Implementing worker&#39;s claim that the shipped build is usable, faithful to approved design and free of target regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder sends its own transcript, modified-file list and completion statement to Architect through runs.jsonl and the synthetic result message. - Complementary access path: Separate QC model role uses the delivered application, renders/screenshots UI, diffs approved mock, tests unusual inputs and writes reproducible findings with IDs independently of worker prose. - Independence boundary: QC has its own configurable model/session and role folder; it cannot edit the build and coders cannot write the QC findings folder under Synapse&#39;s first-party role rules. This is role separation, not tamper-proof institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: Architect reads QC output and delegates the numbered fix to a coder, then assigns verification of that exact repro to QC again, up to the three-round cap. - Function: Independent role-level challenge to implementation claims using real build behavior and a returned quality verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker reports that conceal unusable UI, clipping, misleading states, poor validation or regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: QC chooses and executes adversarial/use-oriented checks, records reproducible findings and re-verifies fixes separately from implementing coders. - Decision owner: Dedicated configured qc model/session, distinct from coder role, with its own findings area and read-only stance toward the build. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QC mandate to render/use the built artifact, screenshot/mock diffs, finding IDs and repros, separate folder permissions, bounded three-round QC/fix/verification plan. - Closure path: QC direct inspection→qcfindings/qc-N.md with evidence→Architect receives report and assigns specific fix task→QC re-runs repros and verifies correction. - Boundary reachability: First-party QC role, delegated real engine session, report loop and role-write restrictions are shipped in Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: QC independently determines and reports actual product defects; the Architect, not QC, retains current-control commitment changes. - Evidence: plans.js#L700, plans.js#L1342, runs.js#L1002. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium: independently tasked source-visible QC path, with no performed cross-provider execution or efficacy audit. - Caveats: The product does not establish strong organizational or cryptographic isolation; different role/model configuration and artifact-based repro constitute the limited claimed independence. If QC is never delegated in a particular plan, the optional path is inactive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Sensing external/future needs and developing options returned to current project capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unanticipated user needs and future features for the product under construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An Inventor model proposes and ranks extra features; operator chooses scope and Architect links confirmed features to tasks. Whether this amounts to an external sensing→adaptation→S3 loop is unresolved. - Decision owner: Inventor generates options and operator accepts features; no proved future-environment evidence trail connecting the two. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Inventor role mandate, optional local search tools, ideas file, confirmed/rejected features.json and Architect planning instructions. - Closure path: Proposed feature→human selection→confirmed feature/task is explicit; source does not establish that the proposal derived from an observed external prospective distinction rather than imagined additions to a current wish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generating project feature ideas is not, alone, a demonstrated VSM S4 process; the active inventor path prevents a defensible categorical absence claim. - Evidence: plans.js#L660, plans.js#L1287, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: bounded uncertainty. - Caveats: Direct evidence of actual external future-condition observation and its governing choice would resolve the state; neither was reproduced at this source pin.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate ultimate-policy or system identity dispute resolution at project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over project identity, ultimate purpose and governing policy rather than current task scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level issue→authority decision→returned governing policy path shown. - Decision owner: Human operator chooses product features, permission and continuity toggles; those remain operational/requirement decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Feature selection forms, prompt constraints, role-specific permission rules and operator approval. - Closure path: Scope selection reaches project plan, but no recognized ultimate-policy decision channel governs a dispute about the system&#39;s identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing which mock to build or whether to continue is not an S5 legitimacy mechanism. - Evidence: plans.js#L1287, automation.js, permissions.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External real-world product governance or developer organizational decisions are out of the assessed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368/assessments/da/darkdvr-synapse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368\">7211b7a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:22:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ArgusAI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai/tree/5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\">5f7aa08 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out security-analysis and general assistant operations through model-selected tools and separate task-performing children. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/traffic observations, new user materials, scan errors, tool outputs, environment and evidence changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and sequence domain tools, analyze returned evidence and choose next investigation/validation/report action. - Decision owner: Configured native model-bearing ArgusAI assistant, or connected model-bearing child where given an explicit bounded role; external ACP engines are not credited as ArgusAI&#39;s independent cognitive design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/tool call loop, domain tool catalog, run/stream state, content archives, confirmation broker and scoped child capability projection. - Closure path: User-defined scope → model/tool call on live workspace → task/evidence output → model inspects and continues/terminates → report or next task state. - Boundary reachability: The native model/tool engine and first-party domain registry are wired into normal GUI/CLI operation, and child tool selection is registered in the shipped assistant. An external ACP model is an alternative supported operating mode, not required for the native witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive task actions; deterministic policy, progress guards and run queues enforce limits rather than owning the choice. - Evidence: assistant loop, tool methods, child execution, README runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for model/tool-path existence; provider execution was not replayed here. - Caveats: A standalone scan engine or GUI table does not own S1 without the configured model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Independently executing persistent Team Room members, each with distinct objective, tool projection, history, inbox and ongoing work; temporary subagents also exist but do not communicate directly. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Repeated peer status chatter loops, duplicate or out-of-order targeted requests, interruption of busy specialists, concurrent competition for shared member capacity and missed handoff dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Team member system context requires model discretion to respond only when its role/objective is implicated, to make concrete @role handoffs and otherwise call teamroompass. Durable inboxes deduplicate input references, queue while busy and preserve explicit request ordering; capacity waiters resume as slots release. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A specialist reviews actual targeted peer/leader messages after finishing its current work, chooses to pass, answer with new evidence or issue an exact-role request; that message enters peer inboxes and changes their next work/action choice. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit model-facing anti-loop rule and selective peer-response/handoff control mitigate inter-agent chatter/attention oscillation, while delivery sequencing/dedup solves concrete concurrent-message interference. A generic chat log alone would not establish the agent-owned decision. - Function: Maintain compatible peer coordination without interrupt/reply storms across simultaneous team work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Redundant chat cycles, missed role-specific requests, reentrant live work and shared member-slot interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide when own role must answer an inter-agent request, pass unproductive chatter, or send a bounded dependency/handoff to another specialist. - Decision owner: Model-bearing persistent member agents exercising model-facing Team Room rules; deterministic queue/dedup/capacity checks only supply delivery discipline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate Team Room log/cursors and pending-input queue, selective @role message route, teamroompass, input reference dedup and member concurrency limits. - Closure path: Peer message/redundant chatter or directed dependency → model member evaluates role fit → pass or targeted correction/handoff → queue records/limits the message → affected member consumes it and adjusts work. - Boundary reachability: Persistent Team Rooms, first-party member prompt and member tools are shipped in the standard GUI/assistant mode; they are not optional third-party skills. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decision whether to escalate/pass/answer is model discretion. Serial/held input alone would not support A, so the positive is restricted to the standing-team model-facing selective coordination path. - Evidence: agent role prompt and member queue, Team Room routing and read cursors, architecture/queue contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no empirical measured chat-loop reduction is claimed. - Caveats: Temporary spawnteam has no peer messaging; this S2 A is confined to the separately supported persistent-team mode, not all single-agent sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Whole-system current view: Parent assistant can read the complete Todo tree, current task/job progress, team roster, child notices, evidence links and tool outputs; owner GUI sees team state and can edit/steer running membership and assignments. - Current-control decision scope: Model chooses investigation phases, work assignments, task continuation, relevant members, tool batches and response to child results. Owner separately changes standing-team staffing, objective/role assignments and active scope of current work, with next-turn effect. - Function: Coordinate immediate work commitments and evidence production for a whole user objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist readiness, task phase dependencies, uncertain evidence and external tool errors, changing user direction, busy child agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model creates/updates whole-goal Todo plan, delegates and redirects workers; parent owner can edit or pause team members, change scope and direct next assigned work. - Decision owner: Main configured model for autonomous management; installation owner via Team GUI/member update for the parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Todo plan, run locks, live UI, child registry, Team Room and group message stores, confirmation/permission gates. - Closure path: Whole-goal evidence and child notices → model changes priorities/delegation → different agent/tool actions; owner observes team, edits member/assignments or sends new instruction → persisted metadata and injected typed event change subsequent child operation. - Boundary reachability: Both assistant model tools (settodos, spawnteam, agent input) and owner GUI team/member edit controls are first-party installed entry points; they return into running agent sessions rather than isolated dashboards. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous model retains current resource choices; the user separately holds a legitimate parent current-control role over the same local team. Hard permissions/queues alone do not count as a second owner. - Evidence: model planning/delegation methods, child control/notifications, owner team/member editing. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high within installed modes; no actual provider-backed orchestration run was executed. - Caveats: User task interjections are not credited alone; the distinct parent mode is the current-control owner GUI plus persistent member state returned to operation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Distinct independent examination of operational claims via complementary raw reality rather than ordinary task reporting; full closure cannot be established confidently from the reviewed default distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading child report, inaccurate scan/finding, incomplete task claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a worker/result with raw Case/Artifact/Tool evidence and return a corrective finding to control; whether a governed non-producer audit owner routinely exercises it is unresolved. - Decision owner: Main assistant may inspect material and can instantiate readonly specialists; the built-in reporter role consolidates findings but is not shown to have a mandatory independently sourced veto/repair channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Case/Evidence original references, readonly subagents, result summaries, operator review, tool histories and durable event logs. - Closure path: Child or domain output → main assistant can reread raw evidence and assign further validation → correction to tasks is possible, but a shipped independent complementary inspection owner and repeatable corrective return are not fully proven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source supports model review as ordinary operations and a purpose-composable review role, yet cannot distinguish a true independent S3 audit from a task-specific secondary analysis in all supported modes. - Evidence: evidence/case store, main review path, readonly specialist presets/child roles. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium for real audit capability, low for verified S3-specific ownership. - Caveats: webaudit reporter summarizes other roles and the human inspects findings; neither automatically supplies independent unfiltered ground-truth inspection. No presumed external security review is credited.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Adapt future patrol priorities, scheduled investigation or long-lived operational capability from external change; specific closed adaptation ownership remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed attack surface, new file/log events, future alerts and cross-session findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and enact a prospective change to future monitoring/adaptation, distinct from simply completing current steps or running fixed Cron commands. - Decision owner: A model may call first-party cronupsert and alter future work triggers; an installation owner can also configure schedules. However no clearly evidenced shipped model process compares new environment trends, decides a durable revised future capability, and closes that decision back into later operating configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cron tasks with interval/cron/webhook/file-change triggers, persisted jobs/run records, long-term session memory and task/Case references. - Closure path: External event → scheduled session inspects current artifacts and may update Todos/schedule → later trigger executes; the decisive model&#39;s prospective adaptation criterion and actual feedback-based reconfiguration are not resolved at this pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model-selectable scheduler plus memory offers a credible first-party adaptation candidate, but scheduling fixed repetitions and reusing prior facts alone are not an established S4 owner. - Evidence: model-visible cronupsert catalog, persistent scheduler, scheduler GUI routes, long-term memory. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for future triggers, insufficient for whether they constitute positive S4 adaptation. - Caveats: ? is caused by an identified plausible shipped path, not by a hypothetical future feature; fixed scheduling should not be mistaken for itself owning outside-and-then intelligence.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What a persistent local Team is for, which named specialist roles constitute it, and which role objectives/tool-power boundaries bind members across sessions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The legitimate self-hosted installation/team owner can create, recharter and edit its standing team and member objectives through first-party GUI/API; no agent-owned constitutional authority to replace that owner&#39;s ultimate charter is established here. - Return-to-operation path: Owner changes standing team purpose or member role/objective/capabilities → persisted team/member record and pending typed metadata update → next member turn receives new role/objective; tool capability projection/confirmation mode enforces limits. - Function: Maintain binding purpose and role identity of the persistent team as a supported parent-governed arrangement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed organizational assignment, durable role constitution and owner-level policy over specialist identity and allowed operating repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively change a team/member&#39;s stated continuing purpose, role identity and authorization scope for subsequent work, beyond changing a single tool call. - Decision owner: Installation owner in the supported human-operated GUI/team setup mode; model tools can request team changes but no distinct self-authorized autonomous ultimate-policy closure is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted Team metadata, member records, edited role/objective, capability projection and queued metadataupdatepending typed event. - Closure path: Parent decides a standing team/role-purpose revision → updateteammember persists the charter and emits event → member&#39;s future context/inbox incorporates it → subsequent agent work follows updated purpose/policy. - Boundary reachability: Creating and updating persistent teams and members are shipped, first-party GUI/API features, not repository maintainer policy or an external integration. The member worker reads revised metadata on its next safe turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited S5 mode is owner-authored standing team constitution, not the assistant approving its own tools or choosing current task steps; S3-level task steering remains separately recorded. - Evidence: persistent team create/update, member metadata revision and next-turn notice, member role/permission API schema. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for the local standing team identity loop. - Caveats: One-off job objectives, approval toggles and permission flags alone are not S5. This claim is limited to the persistent-team role/purpose constitution, not governance of unrelated ArgusAI installations or external security organizations.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd/assessments/ar/argusai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd\">49cc349 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:59:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">colony</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony/tree/b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\">b1a7679 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Adaptively produce software changes and work artifacts for a claimed task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unspecified implementation steps, code reality, failing checks, rebases and local task obstacles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose implementation, tool sequence, code fixes, testing and completion or release of the current claimed task. - Decision owner: Individual model-bearing worker operating inside Colony&#39;s task protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code CLI process, isolated clone, turn prompt, MCP tools, tests, Git hub, budget gate. - Closure path: Current task/goal → worker reads code → chooses changes → checks observed result → repairs or pushes verified work → records task completion. - Boundary reachability: Colony launches real workers with first-party prompt and task tools on its standard command path; model/service availability is external. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model workers choose real next actions and interpret test feedback; the deterministic board does not write code or select solutions. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L80, worker.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for operating ownership; no external provider-backed execution in this review. - Caveats: The coding cognition is supplied by an installed external Claude Code worker; Colony owns the protocol/orchestration that directs it, not the model internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Separate code-writing Claude Code worker processes, each owning a different claimed task and isolated clone. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing task claims, duplicate work, conflicting changes to shared files and unmet task dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workers observe atomic claim feedback and peer messages, change their commitments, reserve dependency-blocked work, negotiate overlap and integrate only after a non-force Git rebase. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A refused claim makes the worker choose another item; a peer mention or rejected push leads to coordination, rebase and retesting before completing its own task. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party protocol connects a named inter-worker collision to its observed refusal or conflict, then instructs the model worker to change its plan and return a non-conflicting commitment. The mechanical lock alone would not suffice. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel workers changing the same goal/repository and return conflict signals to those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing claims, duplicate task proposals, overlapping edits, unmet dependencies and stale peer assumptions can destabilize otherwise productive S1 workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Re-select work after a rejected claim, renegotiate task boundaries via peer chat, revise after conflicting Git changes, or reserve/release an interdependent task. - Decision owner: Peer model workers interpret the conflicting board/chat/Git feedback; board and hub provide non-discretionary exclusivity constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Atomic claim/rejection, duplicate proposal filter, dependency reservation, shared chat/notes, mid-turn inbox and non-force-push shared branch. - Closure path: Worker A claims/changes a task → worker B sees refused claim, chat mention or changed hub → B selects another task, coordinates boundaries or rebases → new board/branch state is visible to both. - Boundary reachability: Multiple workers and board tools are the normal Colony runtime, not an external application constructing its own regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Locks and rebases alone are automatic S2 support; positive attribution is limited to model-owned choice to replan, coordinate and repair after those feedback signals. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L75, board.rs#L391-L562, inbox.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high; the code establishes the regulation loop, not conflict-recovery success rates. - Caveats: When only one worker is started, cross-worker S2 is dormant; low-level locking itself is not counted as agent autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Every worker reads the shared board with all open/claimed/done tasks and the common goal, rather than merely its own tool log. - Current-control decision scope: Any worker can propose missing goal work, amend present task dependencies and commitments, reopen failed completion, or close the whole goal after all current tasks finish and configured checks pass. - Function: Distribute current whole-goal task commitments and regulate unresolved work across the shared colony. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing goal subtasks, blocked critical paths, stale completed work, all-done-but-goal-incomplete conditions and changed availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From a whole-board current view, propose a 5–15-task plan, add missing work, pick/reassign one&#39;s commitment, reopen a flawed completed task, and finally declare goal completion. - Decision owner: Distributed model workers; one agent initially takes the planning task but any worker may amend the plan or exercise whole-goal closure. No separate central director exists. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boardview, shared goal, task dependencies/status, board wakeups, one-planning-task bootstrap, goalcomplete eligibility and optional cached verifier. - Closure path: Goal/current board → worker identifies missing or blocked work → proposes/reopens and changes commitments → colleagues see the revised board and act → terminal whole-goal verification/closure. - Boundary reachability: The shipped standard protocol initializes the planning task and gives every worker whole-board tools and goal-completion rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The status checks are deterministic, but agents decide priorities, propose new current work and judge missing completeness; S3 is distributed rather than embodied by a manager process. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L63, prompts.rs#L89-L99, mcp.rs#L188-L204, board.rs#L852-L890. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no measured claim of effective governance across arbitrary worker counts. - Caveats: No separately privileged S3 agent; the same workers own S1 and distributed S3 choices. Operator-goal changes and budgets remain external.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Claim being audited: A peer&#39;s done task or final shared-branch state meets its acceptance criteria and the colony goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Task owner writes taskcomplete summary with a merged commit; status appears on the board. - Complementary access path: Another worker can inspect the actual shared branch and task details, execute checks, then call taskreopen with a reason; configured verify independently executes an operator-specified test command against the current hub commit. - Independence boundary: Source access and separate peer model process are available but independent reviewer assignment is optional and not enforced; automated verify is a check, not a separate agent judgment. - Who acts on findings: Reopened task becomes claimable and workers are signaled; a failed verify refuses goalcomplete, announces the failure and requires branch repair. - Function: Conditional peer challenge of task completion and reality check of the merged goal state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading done summaries, broken peer changes and unverified whole-goal completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A peer can reject a completion with evidence; optional verifier can block a false goal-complete declaration. - Decision owner: A distinct worker when it undertakes peer inspection; test-command outcome and close-goal gate are deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: taskreopen, source/branch access, task logs, peer notifications, verify commit cache and closure refusal. - Closure path: Owner&#39;s done claim → separate worker inspects actual artifact → reasoned reopen → peer correction/new claim → fresh completion; optional test failure triggers the same operational repair path. - Boundary reachability: Peer-reopen tool and prompt are standard; a different reviewer agent and configured verify command are possible but not required on every completion, warranting C rather than A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The peer can make a substantive source-grounded audit decision; generic testing/board messages are not themselves independent S3 agency. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L89-L99, board.rs#L692-L719, mcp.rs#L269-L285, mcp.rs#L420-L445. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for conditional peer pathway; independent-review coverage not established. - Caveats: taskreopen can be used by the original worker; no source code mandates a distinct auditor. A test failure and its deterministic gate do not prove auditor independence.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced distinct prospective external scanning and organization-level capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current goal implementation, API usage and peer failures, not an evolving future external environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and propagate forward-looking capability/strategy shifts in response to environmental futures; absent. - Decision owner: Operator sets goals, worker count, tools and usage limits; workers can revise within-goal plans only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan task T1, peer notes, repeated sessions, usage-limit throttling and dynamic current tasks. - Closure path: Within-goal task replanning returns to present operations; no prospective option evaluation leading to durable new capability and authorized integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing tomorrow&#39;s step of an already fixed software goal is current execution, not S4 environmental adaptation. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L102, supervisor.rs#L1-L14. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for reviewed first-party modes. - Caveats: A user may assign a future-oriented project goal; task subject matter does not establish a distinct harness S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No harness-owned identity/ultimate-policy governance that can legitimately redefine the binding goal and return a constitutional decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-configured budget, deadlines, permissions and Git restrictions are operational bounds, not identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve binding-purpose/policy conflicts with authorized parent return; absent. - Decision owner: Operator supplies goal and budget, Rust supervisor enforces stops, and workers choose within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal text, usage caps, task/branch gates, deterministic verifier and human external CLI configuration. - Closure path: Worker goal-complete stops the job and budget stop cancels workers; neither authorizes a change of organizational identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A worker&#39;s finishing declaration is current objective closure (S3), not binding S5 purpose adjudication. - Evidence: supervisor.rs, mcp.rs#L420-L445, prompts.rs#L38-L46. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Operator authorization can govern the colony from outside the reviewed first-party system; no internal legitimate parent role is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d/assessments/co/colony.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d\">387aebd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:05:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Leadkit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit/tree/3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\">3efcde5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement project-scoped coding and verification tasks through agent-selected reading, editing, testing and tool use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable code requests, test outcomes, repository content and task-specific errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the installed developer/specialist Claude Code Agent decides how to perform its assigned task and reacts to tool observations and reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: active model-driven role Agent in Claude Code Teams, configured by Leadkit&#39;s shipped first-party role definitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code model/tool host, installed role allowlist, task files, path guard, queue/status verbs and project profile. - Closure path: tools return output to the active agent; it changes/tests code, records evidence and returns its result to task/reviewer queue. - Boundary reachability: standard adoption installs the role definitions and wires hooks; the deployed Host Agent Teams directly consumes these, whereas bare leadkit CLI-only execution is explicitly not claimed to be autonomous. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role agent chooses substantive code edits and validation steps; CLI/guards enforce boundaries and do not generate the operational choices. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/developer.md#L1-L40; internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L85; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L1-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural within adopted supported mode. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: model execution belongs to external Claude Code; no live paid Claude Code Teams E2E was performed, and disabled Teams yields no autonomous team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: lead-assigned developer and specialist Agents working potentially overlapping git paths and successive reviews, each with its own task/owner identity. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent edits to the same path can silently undo peer work; premature parallel critique can review a SHA invalidated by prior code-review fixes; orphaned task ownership can stall delivery. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead Agent deliberately assigns path ownership, sequences the code-review and critique sessions, returns failures to original owner, and orchestrator Agent resolves stale/idle queue handoffs. Path/queue and gate checks enforce these decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: reviewer findings change the owner&#39;s next implementation, a changed commit invalidates old approval, and queue/orphan status causes reassignment or escalation rather than duplicate work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these mechanisms target concrete shared-path collision, stale-SHA review race and lost-ownership hazards and return corrective outcomes to specific work units. - Function: attenuate interference among parallel project Agents so ownership and review outcomes remain consistent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file contention, role drift, stale review race, lost work and duplicate ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead chooses paths and reviewer order; the orchestrator chooses how to handle queue stalls within documented roles; deterministic planner provides constraints and signals. - Decision owner: model-driven Lead/Orchestrator Agents in the shipped enabled-team operating mode, not the Go planner or filesystem alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership guard, role lanes, task IDs, deterministic plan, reviewer chain SHA binding and distinct identities. - Closure path: owner assignment enters task store/queue, agents act within scoped paths, review findings return to builder, and next verified SHA advances review/ship stages. - Boundary reachability: these roles and paths are installed through the public adoption workflow and use active Claude Code Teams Agent/SendMessage capabilities, not only project maintainer dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the meaningful assignment and remediation selection belongs to autonomous role Agents; guards deny inconsistent actions but do not choose the route. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/agents/orchestrator.md#L1-L28; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L1-L100; internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/gate/chain.go#L65-L140. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for standard deployed role protocol. - Caveats: coordination relies on following first-party role instructions; the Go CLI does not itself launch or monitor a proprietary model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Whole-system current view: project queue/status/owner/priority/dependency records plus live roster passed in from Agent Teams; account burn telemetry, workstream count, budget reserve and pause-lock status. - Current-control decision scope: choose active team workload and concurrency, defer dispatch when budget is stressed, prioritize critical tasks, requeue orphaned work or grant owner-directed staffing/priority changes. - Function: maintain present project-team commitments, workload and throughput within user resource and authority bounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misallocated role capacity, budget burn, stuck tasks and competing current priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party planner exposes S3-specific route/requeue/hold/triage choices, while the installed autonomous actor must assemble a live roster and act on higher-level capacity/priority reports; in the parent mode the human selects the active roster/priority/parallelism and authorizes changes. - Decision owner: C constructor has deterministic CLI and Agent-facing role protocol but lacks a self-contained autonomous S3 governor with all decisive rights; P mode is the legitimate project operator who decides and applies current resource/priority constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: leadkit status, burn/priority planner, usage history, account pause lock, role roster, task queue, apply confirmation, waker/sweep daemons. - Closure path: computed route/hold/escalation is returned to orchestrator or human; accepted priority/roster/budget decisions alter task status or available consumers, and subsequent queue/burn samples expose the result. - Boundary reachability: CLI+roles+hooks/daemons are standard installed interfaces, including explicit owner changes, not private governance; C still needs external Agent Teams and operator adoption, P needs actual human choice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: consuming a deterministic burn verdict and routing plan does not by itself make the autonomous Lead own a whole-system capacity tradeoff. Parent control is a distinct explicit operating mode rather than a decoration on an approval button. - Evidence: internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/orchestrate/apply.go#L1-L80; cmd/leadkit/cmdorchestrate.go#L1-L110; internal/burn/burn.go#L1-L115; internal/burn/throttle.go#L1-L70; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium; project-specific actual authorization and agent roster are deployment-specific. - Caveats: automatic pause/resume is deterministic enforcement; the C notation is restricted to S3-specific live-resource decision paths, not arbitrary queue commands. Parent mode presumes a real operator uses reports to revise priority/roster rather than merely installing a static config.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: a code diff at a pinned SHA is correct/safe and actually serves the ticket&#39;s intended goal. - Ordinary reporting path: builder Agent&#39;s completion claim, code changes and test logs. - Complementary access path: separate read-only code-review and critique Agents inspect diff, task and surrounding code under different questions and record own SHA-bound verdicts/evidence. - Independence boundary: distinct installed roles/tools (reviewers lack Edit/Write/SendMessage), order and different-role gate checks; reviewers are not the builder and may not file the other layer&#39;s verdict. - Who acts on findings: original builder/lead receives changes-needed; gate blocks push/PR path until matching independent ready layers, then finalise/human may proceed. - Function: complementary audit of operational output and purpose before a consequential code shipment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-certified implementation errors, wrong goal, stale-SHA approval, and review-role collusion/impersonation accidents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate model-driven reviewer Agents make substantive correctness and goal-fit judgments, each recorded on observed commit evidence. - Decision owner: reviewer Agents own their respective judgments, while Go gate and forge enforcement decide only whether required current, role-separated records exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer read-only grants, mutation-evidence notes, chain/order, SHA stamps, status/queue, push hooks and optional verified server protection. - Closure path: changes-needed returns work to developer, revised SHA invalidates old approvals, both ready verdicts permit finalise/push within granted authority. - Boundary reachability: reviewer roles and gate are shipped/installable first-party components in the normal team setup, not merely CI tests; the external Claude Code host executes reviewers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic findings belong to independent review Agents; checking SHA/order/identity and command gating is deterministic enforcement. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/code-review-agent.md#L1-L65; internal/starter/agents/critique-agent.md#L1-L85; internal/gate/chain.go#L1-L140; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L35-L100; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party role separation and gate. - Caveats: recorded --by identity may be self-asserted when host does not bind CLAUDEAGENTNAME; local hooks are bypassable without forge protection. Multiple reviewers may share provider/model family; independence is role/evidence separation, not externally certified audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced external-future sensing, adaptation-option judgment and return into current project capability at the assessed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emerging technology/organizational threats or future opportunities are not a first-party recurrent object of control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 actor chooses strategic redesign from external signals and deploys it. - Decision owner: not established; humans may modify roles or open issues; normal Lead coordination operates on present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feedback-as-issues in overarching-lead protocol, role capture, postmortems, repo governance and backlog. - Closure path: issues preserve potential improvements but no closed strategic adoption loop into team capabilities is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks and capturing lessons is not necessarily outside-and-then adaptation. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L100; README.md#L75-L145. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user can commission tool upgrades; they remain discretionary external work, not a shipped S4 mode. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether a project team may grant itself standing rights to push, create PRs, merge or modify protected repository policy, versus retaining those fundamental authorization rights at the repository owner. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: direct project/repository owner alone; peer lead and overarching coordinator cannot grant or relay authority in lieu of the owner. - Return-to-operation path: owner-approved authority.&lt;repo&gt; entry in the project profile changes guards and governed PR/push commands; protected-branch rules can be installed and read back, and subsequent operations are permitted/blocked by the returned grant. - Function: reserve ultimate scope of consequential agent action to direct human owner authority and apply it as a durable team constitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent self-grant, laundering authority through a peer coordinator, unreviewed merges/pushes and inconsistent rights across repositories. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or deny standing repository mutation/merge authority, and amend protected-branch policy under owner mandate. - Decision owner: legitimate human project/repository owner in the shipped parent-governed setup, not the autonomous Lead or other session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-repo authority profile, adoption stop point, PR/push guard, default-deny allowlists, forge protection verification and doctor. - Closure path: team must request direct owner grant; owner decision is written to profile/forge policy; subsequent hook/CLI operations consume it, permit only granted action and report denials. Denial leaves default-deny intact. - Boundary reachability: authority-grant step and policy guards are part of normal adoption flow and CLI; code explicitly reads named per-repo authority, so the mode is real without relying on GitHub contributors as a metasystem. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents cannot legitimately establish their own standing constitutional authority merely by relaying a request; the owner remains the sole source of grants, while Go enforces the returned decision. - Evidence: docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105; internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; README.md#L75-L145; docs/design/agent-sandboxing-and-enforcement.md#L1-L95; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural parent-governed closure. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party policy path, not for an unseen specific live owner decision. - Caveats: ordinary per-PR human review is not counted as S5; standing authority is the constitutional object. Client-side hooks alone are bypassable; host/forge enforcement and real owner-supplied profile must be configured.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4/assessments/le/leadkit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4\">a4ff63c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T18:09:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Recurse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse/tree/6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\">6e4d231 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce code/repository outcomes through independently model-directed persistent work on a task, including observation, edits, tests, terminal commands and iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, command/test failures, incomplete context, long-running commands and interruptions/restarts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each agent chooses its subsequent tools, edits, tests, recovery, further child tasks or final result from observed feedback. - Decision owner: the Pi Durable model actor in each root/child conversation, subject to external operator authorization and safety-tool constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent AgentNode, Durable Harness, background shell/wait/monitor, persisted messages, working-directory state and safety checks. - Closure path: operator/parent task → model-selected tools/edits → shell/file/agent observations and completion wakes → subsequent model decision and result report; durable work can resume across browser disconnection and restart. - Boundary reachability: normal recurse /path starts a real agent; documented subagent create starts more independent model agents from CLI without a special development fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model turns choose task-level actions and responses; deterministic process supervision only transports results and gates execution. - Evidence: quickstart and purpose, runtime guide, Durable turns. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running implementer child agents with separate model turns, local tasks and repository-editing decisions, under one parent tree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous implementation in one checkout can overwrite, interleave or destabilize another child&#39;s in-progress file changes; the official guide explicitly motivates separate worktrees to prevent this shared-write interference. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent model assigns separate named managed Git worktrees during subagent create --worktree, so concurrent implementers operate against isolated working files and branches; parent/worker exchange and eventual review/merge reconcile outcomes rather than expose the same working tree to competing writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the chosen worktree path becomes the spawned child&#39;s effective working directory for its later tool calls; its branch or PR comes back as an integration artifact, and the parent can message/reassign a child or defer/alter a merge when work overlaps. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the intervention targets a named code-edit collision between distinct S1 producers; it changes their actual workspace topology and future write targets. Messaging alone, ordinary task splitting or generic queueing is not credited. - Function: damp parallel implementation interference by deciding and maintaining task-specific checkout separation and integrating branch-level results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent editing races and inadvertent shared-workspace corruption; downstream logical Git merge conflicts remain possible. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which child receives a fresh checkout and task/branch, and adjust work allocation or subsequent merge handling after reports. - Decision owner: parent model-led agent when operating the documented parallel-worktree mode; deterministic Git/worktree holder machinery implements the isolation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent.ts --worktree, worktree.ts named checkouts and holder claims, per-agent cwd, Git branches, child messages/final reports. - Closure path: parent recognizes parallel edits → assigns separate worktrees → each child modifies its own checkout → branch/PR or feedback returns → parent selects review, integration, clarification or further task allocation; future child work remains separated. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI and runtime support worktree-bearing spawns; the first-party guide explicitly recommends and illustrates this normal multi-agent delivery pattern, not an unimplemented suggestion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model elects when/how to separate work and which tasks/branches to assign; static worktree creation, holder bookkeeping and Git merge mechanics are enforcement, not autonomous coordinators. - Evidence: worktrees, multiagent and collision purpose, child creation interface, managed checkouts and claims, example agent graph. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: there is no automatic semantic merge-conflict arbitrator; S2 here is model-selected preventive checkout isolation and returned branch handling, not guaranteed conflict-free integration. One-agent or shared-cwd use does not exercise it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the parent&#39;s model-request snapshot lists its child/descendant status and queued inputs, while native subagent reads/waits/messages expose individual work and results; the supervisor maintains authoritative live membership and lifecycle status. - Current-control decision scope: the parent can choose/constrain concurrent commitments and effort on creation, interrupt or redirect active children, reassign with move, retire stale work cells and integrate or rework their output as the joint goal demands. - Function: govern a coding tree&#39;s ongoing resource/commitment allocation and exceptional intervention while independent implementers own their local tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled agents, poor distribution of effort, late user requirements, active conflicts or incomplete child tasks and divergent branch outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to launch, interrupt, redirect, reparent or retire child work and whether its returned work satisfies the whole task. - Decision owner: model-backed parent agent in the supported multiagent tree; operator may intervene or take over, but operator interaction is not assumed to constitute an independent whole-team S3 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live Supervisor/tree projection, agent digest, subagent.ts create/send/interrupt/wait/move/rm, Durable report holds and completion delivery, fixed resource/process limits. - Closure path: status/child output/monitor/user steer → parent sees current tree information → parent selects task or resource intervention → supervisor/CLI changes relevant child agent and sends new input → changed work and updated status return to parent. - Boundary reachability: the agent prompt includes a live tree digest; the shipped CLI supports the control operations under normal local execution, and child reports reach the parent automatically. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model decides interventions and current tradeoffs; the supervisor enforces ownership/fencing and routes commands but is not itself a discretionary S3 agent. Merely having a class named Supervisor is insufficient. - Evidence: parent model tree digest, CLI control surface, tree ownership and interventions, child report closure. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party standard operating audit process is evidenced that independently inspects completed operational results and returns findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic code-completion claims, faulty edits or unsafe commands could require independent checking. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect raw delivered artifacts outside ordinary child reporting and issue a complementary verdict with corrective return; no installed closed path established. - Decision owner: pi-safety uses a separate model to grant/deny proposed actions; an optional reviewer child can be authored by a user, but there is no default complementary outcome auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate model safety reviewer, action transcript, built-in denial gate, recipe for a review-agent graph, source tests and developer review rules. - Closure path: safety&#39;s pre-execution DENY returns a reason and can change the next actor proposal; this regulates permissions, not independent observation of the action&#39;s real outcome. User-authored review scripts may create further loops but are not established here as an active standard process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the second model genuinely judges proposed tool authorization, but its evidence deliberately excludes tool outputs, edited file content and post-execution result; its separate model identity does not make it complementary operational audit. - Evidence: review evidence boundary and fail-open behavior, user-composed reviewer example, agent report path. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct prospective environment-model and capability adaptation loop is established beyond ordinary coding tasks and event-driven continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider/tool ecosystems, CI trends, new project requirements and future architectural possibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a new whole-harness capability based on external/future distinctions and carry it back into live operation; not evidenced. - Decision owner: agents can investigate and change repository code when tasked, while users/developers configure tools and skills; no autonomous organization-level S4 owner is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: background monitors, memory, MCP configuration, skills, models and task-local planning. - Closure path: a CI/deployment monitor can wake an agent for immediate handling, but a signal-to-task reaction is not itself prospective option formation and a capability-adaptation return to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future code steps is S1 task choice; Recurse&#39;s extensibility offers means to construct S4 but no first-party defined future/environment adaptation decision loop. - Evidence: monitor and goals examples, extensibility principles, runtime guide. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudicator is established for the multiagent coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky commands, operator intent conflicts, policy exceptions, agent takeovers and security-mode choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle a whole-organization identity or ultimate-policy tension and return a binding new rule; no closed function-specific path shown. - Decision owner: operator/developer owns Recurse permissions and safety instructions; model safety reviewer applies predefined action policy, and child/parent agents follow ordinary delegated task authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user task prompts, first-party pi-safety and custom additive policy rules, operator-only takeover, tool denial circuit breaker and user consent for exact actions. - Closure path: model reviewer may return DENY and request a safer action or explicit authorization; that decides an individual command under existing policy, not a new identity-governing rule for the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model can reason about dangerous operations, but ultimate authority to change policy is not delegated to a live S5 body with a policy-level issue/escalation and operational return. - Evidence: safety review limits, operator takeover, takeover state/authority. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d/assessments/re/recurse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d\">e94effa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:07:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CJVigil/Orco\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orco</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CJVigil/Orco/tree/148f21d5a4dcf4f833e87d9fa38d3a1bfbe9df59\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 148f21d5a4dcf4f833e87d9fa38d3a1bfbe9df59\">148f21d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform substantive software/project tasks through configured model-bearing connected agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user requirements, source code state, failing tool results, changed ticket context and incomplete information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose investigations, tool invocations, file modifications, responses, task completion and when to ask for input. - Decision owner: Connected model agent using the launched CLI and MCP tools; the human initiating work owns the original request, not the agent&#39;s operational choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task claim leases, connected bridge, model CLI adapter, prompt/context builder, local workspace, MCP task state and policy/budget gates. - Closure path: User/other agent requests work → bridge claims task and launches model → model reads context/uses tools and adjusts work to observed results → updatetask done/blocked/failed updates shared state. - Boundary reachability: The shipped orco connect bridge actually starts configured local models from assigned tasks; the default bridge prompt and installed Claude-code skill prescribe this task-to-action lifecycle, rather than relying on the maintainer&#39;s development agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic orchestration delivers requests and enforces limits; the model chooses substantive work under those limits. - Evidence: bridge default/runner, model adapter, task API. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for wired agent decision path; medium for behavior of the external model runtime. - Caveats: No live external model credentials or end-to-end provider sessions were exercised in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent parallel connected operational agents from modifying an incompatible shared resource at the same time, with feedback for changed agent behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two simultaneously working agents both attempt to use the same branch, deployment target, migration or another named shared project resource. - Decisive decision or feedback right: On resource conflict, choose wait, release/retry later or pick another task; decide when the shared resource can be relinquished. - Decision owner: The involved model-bearing task agents in the shipped bridge prompt / integration skill. Deterministic lock service owns exclusion and fencing, not the discretionary response to denied access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project-scoped named lock holder, TTL, monotonically advanced fence, conflict response identifying holder and expiry, blocking wait helper and explicit acquire/release MCP tools. - Closure path: Agent A obtains named lock → agent B sees conflict identifying holder/expiry → shipped model guidance makes B wait or change work rather than collide → lock expiry/release and subsequent acquire permit resumed work. - Boundary reachability: The bridge&#39;s bundled DEFAULTPROMPT explicitly requires acquirelock/releaselock before touching shared resources; the distributed Claude-code Orco skill explicitly specifies waiting or picking different work after a conflict. Both are provided for ordinary supported agent connections. - Distinct S1 units: Separate connected coding agents with independent task assignments and local model processes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous work on a single branch, deployment target or migration. - Attenuating coordination relation: Mutual-exclusion leases plus a returned lock-conflict signal that the model uses to alter its next activity. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The denied model changes task/order or waits until the holder releases, so following tool actions use a nonconflicting shared resource. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The named resource collision is concrete inter-agent interference; first-party lock acquisition, conflict disclosure and instructed behavioral change close a specific attenuation cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lock arithmetic and fencing are runtime enforcement, whereas agents choose the amended work schedule after feedback. - Evidence: locks, bridge prompt, installed Orco skill. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for standard connected agents and actual conflict return. - Caveats: Lock discipline is advisory for code execution outside Orco; a noncompliant agent can bypass these project-scoped locks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: View current work commitments and failures across managed agents/projects and change live allocation, task priorities and intervention decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled agent tasks, blocked tickets, overlapping priorities, waiting approvals, CI failure, overspend and missed current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which current work to reprioritize, pause, assign, unblock or escalate in light of portfolio/project state. - Decision owner: Base constructor mode: a separately configured model supervisor asked by the operator to use the shipped projectplan, listtickets and updateticket tools (C); no such supervisor is installed by default. Parent mode: authenticated human org/project administrator viewing portfolio health and changing tickets/pauses/budgets (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Server-generated project plan and ticket summaries, cross-project health/roadmap/CI/approval/budget aggregation, ticket mutation API, scoped pause/budget control and audit events. - Closure path: Base: constructed model supervisory session reads whole project open work and plan → decides reassignment/priority update → first-party ticket edits change connected agents&#39; subsequent commitments. Parent: portfolio displays blocked/stalled/over-budget agents and tasks → admin decides assignment/priority/pause → persisted controls/tickets alter later bridge task starts and available work. - Boundary reachability: First-party MCP ships the necessary project-wide read/update tools for a constructor-configured agent mode, while the first-party Orco human UI and controls route expose portfolio current state and authorized admin changes in normal self-hosted deployment; neither requires maintainer CI or a private service. - Whole-system current view: For the base constructor, the accessible plan, tickets and dependencies of the selected Orco project (not a complete cross-instance view); for the parent, Portfolio.overview aggregates all visible projects&#39; tickets, stalled agents, approvals, CI, budgets and milestone risk. - Current-control decision scope: Assigning/updating task commitments, priority and workflow status, pausing selected agents/projects or all agents, and modifying operational budget limits in response to present problems. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime computes health and enforces controls but does not select a supervisory response. An operator must construct an agent manager to own that discretionary S3 right; the shipped human-admin mode closes it without an autonomous manager. - Evidence: portfolio, MCP project/ticket actions, tickets, runtime controls. - Basis: structural + explicit - Confidence: medium for constructor eligibility, high for human-admin closure. - Caveats: C does not assert a first-party always-on autonomous supervisor. The portfolio view itself is informational until a legitimate admin/model decision changes work.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A distinct connected review agent independently evaluates another agent&#39;s concrete work and returns corrective findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent may declare a ticket complete despite defects or missing acceptance requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review the actual ticket/workspace and choose approved or changesrequested with an explanatory note. - Decision owner: A separately addressed model-bearing reviewer, not the original task agent. Human reviewers are also supported, but not used to upgrade agent ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Requester != reviewer identity check, separate reviewer task and bridge wake, review status persistence, ticket review/rework transition and task restart. - Closure path: Task agent requests review of ticket → separate reviewer agent receives a new explicit task to inspect the work and submit verdict → changesrequested writes review note, moves ticket to rework and starts original agent&#39;s work again; an agent approval records assessment while final done remains gated to a person. - Boundary reachability: Reviews.request creates a real task for a named agent reviewer through first-party Tasks.create, which the shipped connected bridge executes; Reviews.submit directly implements rework and restart. - Claim being audited: Work correctness and acceptance for a particular Orco task/ticket. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker submits updatetask done or ticket progress in its own thread. - Complementary access path: A different agent&#39;s model run has a separate task identity, can read the project ticket/context and inspect actual workspace through its own configured coding CLI. - Independence boundary: Reviewer cannot be the requester; it holds a different Orco participant identity and process. Institutional independence, different model provider and read-only file permissions are not guaranteed. - Who acts on findings: The review subsystem moves the ticket to rework and starts the assigned coding agent again with the review note attached. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A reviewing LLM exercises the verdict; deterministic review/ticket machinery transmits the judgment and restarts work. - Evidence: reviews request, verdict and restart, bridge, ticket review path. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for optional first-party reviewer mode. - Caveats: Review is not mandatory for every task. A separate agent identity alone does not prove critical rigor or provider independence; review quality is unmeasured here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped closed cycle that senses external/prospective change, selects adaptation to future capability and returns that selection to current operational repertoire. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future milestone risk, new follow-up work noticed during tasks and recurring job demand are recorded, but these are not independently established as future environment intelligence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge external/future environmental alternatives and adopt a changed capability or operating repertoire; not established. - Decision owner: Humans manage milestones, accept follow-up suggestions and approve schedules; ordinary connected agents can propose work and schedules but do not hold a shipped adaptation mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Portfolio cross-project roadmap, dated milestones, agent suggestions with optional owner-authorized ticket filing, scheduled recurring tasks and human approvals. - Closure path: Agent can suggest a follow-up → owner accepts into backlog (or an allowed automatic ticket is filed), and scheduled tasks can later run; no documented installed step converts future outside-environment analysis into a selected capability adaptation that returns to operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Backlog intake, forecast risk labels and repeat execution do not alone imply outside-and-then strategy or a governed capability change. - Evidence: suggestions, schedules, portfolio roadmap, MCP milestone operations. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium, bounded to the shipped first-party standard distribution. - Caveats: A human organization using Orco can implement strategic analysis externally; no universal claim against that larger system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Legitimate human parent authority establishes organization-wide and project-specific enduring operating identity/rules and returns binding decisions to subsequent agent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting agent freedoms, project-local exceptions, unsafe spending/deploy authority and mismatch between organizational mandate and project execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Define or revise authoritative organizational/project rules, required approvals, allowed operational agents and persistent task-budget policy; subordinate projects may tighten but may not relax org constraints. - Decision owner: Authenticated human organization administrator or project administrator; first-party Policies.set refuses agents and applies scope checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted org/project policy, mergePolicy inheritance/tightening, agent bridge prompt policy lines, new-task admission and budget validation, audit trail and controls event. - Closure path: Admin receives governance need and sets org/project policy → it is persisted and scoped into the effective project mandate → new task creation rejects forbidden agents/excess budgets; later bridge prompts include binding rules and approval requirements → subsequent agent operation reflects the decision. - Boundary reachability: The first-party self-hosted server exposes Policies.set to authenticated administrators and directly calls effective policy at task admission and rulesFor in connected-agent contexts. This path is shipped, not an assumed external user-written AGENTS.md. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which agents and actions the ongoing project/organization authorizes, which approvals remain nondelegable and what spending or time rights every new agent task receives. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: In the one credited parent mode, org administrator sets binding server-wide rules and project administrator sets only narrower local rules; the policy setter rejects model agents outright. - Return-to-operation path: Stored administrator decision is combined into effective policy; new-task admission enforces the eligible agents and budget caps, and subsequent connected-agent task context receives policy rules and approval requirements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Only a legitimate human admin has authority to change the continuing constitution; agents execute within its returned constraints. Discretionary governance remains at the parent recursion. - Evidence: policies, bridge context and rules, task admission. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for hierarchy/admission/prompt return; lower for compliance with textual rules by an external model. - Caveats: Plain-language policy obedience depends on provider behavior; allowed agent lists and budget caps have stronger deterministic enforcement.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/670ef2c13fe29c9a7f414b48b2182b4727b90970/assessments/or/orco.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 670ef2c13fe29c9a7f414b48b2182b4727b90970\">670ef2c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:46:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/splashxmoon/friday\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Friday</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/splashxmoon/friday/tree/7db025c6513eae1c05a93f6e7ba58fd5ffad2a2a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7db025c6513eae1c05a93f6e7ba58fd5ffad2a2a\">7db025c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an operator&#39;s work objective into code, tests, documents or other concrete repository artifacts and correct defects according to tool/source feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requirements, codebase differences, unexpected test results and task-local implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the assigned model-driven lane chooses substantive implementation/tool calls, interprets outcomes and revises its own artifact before reporting completion. - Decision owner: a Friday-managed coding-agent lane invoking its configured external Claude Code, Codex, other CLI or Friday Code model; not the UI, deterministic task allocator or model vendor&#39;s unrelated organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session state, local CLI adapter and worktree, role instructions, checkpoints, task board, test runner, manual permission mode and event history. - Closure path: human/lead task → owned lane executes substantive model/tool steps → workspace and check feedback → revised files/report → task complete or returned for rework. - Boundary reachability: README standard Code and Crew paths, src/runner/runner.ts run/turn interface, src/agent-mesh.ts assign/claim/finish and src/teammates.ts role handoff are shipped first-party integration paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven lane leaves only launch, log and gate code; that code cannot determine source-specific implementation by itself. - Evidence: pinned README, src/runner/runner.ts, src/agent-mesh.ts, src/teammates.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for owned role and executable paths, not for every vendor CLI combination&#39;s observed quality. - Caveats: provider CLI agents and models remain external execution dependencies; their internal agent hierarchies are not attributed to Friday.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among simultaneously editing worker lanes by assigning file custody and returning conflict warnings to the agent before further edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two otherwise independent agent lanes claiming or editing the same file, incompatible interface changes and stale board/task ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept non-overlapping claimfiles paths; reject claims already owned by other lanes; on a conflicting diff, instruct the colliding worker to stop editing and consult or wait for the holder. - Decision owner: constructor-defined Friday mesh claim/ownership checks; subsequent peer negotiation is by task actors, not evidence of a dedicated autonomous S2 policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: files.held, files.released, board owns sets, heldBy, onDiff, agent messages and wake events. - Closure path: first lane holds path → second lane claims or changes it → Friday identifies holder and refuses claim or warns the violator → second lane must stop and coordinate or wait before continuing. - Boundary reachability: agent-visible claimfiles and releasefiles tools and automatic onDiff are part of shipped src/agent-mesh.ts integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination relation reaches worker behavior, but collision arbitration is a prewritten deterministic rule; peer dialogue alone does not prove an independent S2 agent making the binding priority policy. - Distinct S1 units: independent Friday-managed coding-agent lanes working in the same session/repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: conflicting writes/claims to the same source path or a board-owned file. - Attenuating coordination relation: ownership claim, refused second claim and holder-specific warning on changed files. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: collision message explicitly directs the second worker to stop changing the file, ask the owner, or wait for release. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it detects and returns a concrete cross-worker collision, rather than merely listing agents or assigning tasks. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (holdFiles, releaseFiles, onDiff, heldBy) and README team-worktree instructions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the implemented narrow path. - Caveats: collision warning and cooperative file claiming are advisory; no proof that arbitrary external tools cannot violate the instructions or that the entire repository has transactional file locks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: an authorized lead agent manages present board commitments, assignment, current blocked work, reallocation and completion under an operator&#39;s goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled team progress, uneven available capacity, failed checks/review, agents running out of usage, inconsistent task progress and unclear priorities across active lanes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the designated lead model can autonomously create/decompose a board, assign work, steer or stop a teammate in Crew mode, cancel/retry/reassign blocked tasks, inspect reports and decide the next current step. - Decision owner: the configured first-party-managed lead agent inside a Friday Code/Crew session; Friday enforces role permissions, and the human owner still has higher-level intervention rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guarded posttasks, assigntask, managetask, steeragent; dependency/cycle checks, finite board, event projection, current budget/usage signals, deterministic dispatch, retries and lead reports. - Closure path: goal/current workload and progress evidence → lead model posts or adjusts the board/steers worker → Friday dispatches its authorized decision → reports/tests/usage/blocks return to lead → lead revises assignments or calls for remediation. - Boundary reachability: src/agent-mesh.ts offers board management to the lead and reserves steering to state.lead; src/team-selfrun.ts explicitly tells an enabled autonomous lead to choose the next valuable tasks, hand them out, decide open questions, and stop only at goal completion/budget/stall. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-work authority is not only deterministic queue management; an explicitly designated model-driven lead can choose and alter task partition and reprioritization through role-gated tools. This positive state requires the supported lead/Crew path, not passive solo-agent UI mode. - Whole-system current view: one session/room&#39;s board statuses, owners, checks, message reports, agent availability, usage limits and budget, bounded by the human&#39;s goal. - Current-control decision scope: board admission and redistribution, teammate redirection and when to try/stop/rework; not a demonstrated corporate-level resource or identity authority. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (manageTask, postTasks, control, steer, round, reportBoard) and src/team-selfrun.ts; README Crew path. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party-owned agent lead control mode. - Caveats: whether the chosen provider model actually exercises that discretion well is not observed; deterministic budgets and automated selection are C components within this A ownership claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: ask a separate model-driven reviewer to challenge a worker&#39;s own completion claim against its actual artifacts, task requirements and recorded proof, and return necessary corrections before board completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unverified self-reported completion, missed requirements and defects not caught by the worker&#39;s own tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a different available reviewer lane reads another agent&#39;s task, report, proof and diff/source and returns a substantive approval or correction verdict. - Decision owner: the independently assigned model-driven reviewer lane, distinct from the task&#39;s implementing lane. Friday&#39;s check runner and verdict parser are support/gates rather than the substantive audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewOrPass, different-lane pickReviewer, awayDiff, proof/changes handoff, board.reviewed, bounded rework, configured tests, optional model-based goalRequest judge. - Closure path: worker reports finished task + actual diff/proof → Friday runs its checks → if eligible, separate reviewer sees task and evidence → reviewer decides pass or correction → failed verdict sends defects back to worker and repeats review, or terminal escalation after rework bound. - Boundary reachability: the standard multi-agent board review path src/agent-mesh.ts dispatches to a lane other than t.lane; onRunEnded records its verdict, and rework returns rejected findings. A separate task goal judge is available under src/goal.ts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the supported second-review path, the reviewer model reads changed artifacts and makes a case-specific challenge that code cannot predefine. Automatic checks alone, a UI read-only panel, and an operator&#39;s final review are not counted as autonomous S3. - Claim being audited: a specific assigned board task is correctly finished and fits acceptance/requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing lane&#39;s completion report, test commands and worktree changes. - Complementary access path: Friday presents the other reviewer source/worktree or the recorded diff and task proof, with independent reviewer instructions and test receipts rather than merely trusting the author&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent lane, not guaranteed different model vendor or fully isolated model context; source access depends on whether the reviewer runs on the same machine or receives a recorded diff. - Who acts on findings: mesh sends failed verdict and concrete instructions back to the original task lane for bounded repair; successful review unlocks dependent tasks. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (runChecks, reviewOrPass, pickReviewer, round, boardRunEnded, rework, awayDiff), src/team-proof.ts, src/goal.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high in configured multi-agent second-review mode. - Caveats: Friday deliberately skips cross-review for qualifying small changes with real passing tests, no changed files or unavailable alternate reviewer; thus S3=A is conditional, not universal. Vendor/model or context independence is not proven beyond different lanes and evidence paths.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated first-party decision loop that turns anticipated external developments into changes of the Friday work organization&#39;s future mission/capability envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changed tool limits, learned model effectiveness, later repo errors and new scheduled tasks are sensed, but the cited response is route optimization or current execution within existing permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model/tool selection, replayed lessons and human-accepted reusable skills do not by themselves constitute durable first-party prospective-capability choice. - Decision owner: constructor-defined scoring rules and human skill approver for the observed adaptation mechanisms; no bounded autonomous Friday S4 organizational actor identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: route-learning feedback grades, model benchmark priors, routing.ts choice, Crew skill proposals and human acceptance, memory/automation setup. - Closure path: observed test/review feedback → model-fit estimate/current routing or proposed skill → later present task; no environmental forecast and S4 option evaluation leading to an authorized changed future organizational capability is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a feedback-driven heuristic and task-level learning are not enough to assign future-facing organization intelligence; even autonomous self-run leads act on the current user goal and board. - Evidence: pinned src/route-learning.ts, src/routing.ts, src/team-skills.ts, src/team-selfrun.ts, README memory/automation sections. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a denial of ordinary learning, future-oriented user projects, or the possibility that a parent organization uses Friday as its S4 tool.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported ultimate-identity or constitutional-policy adjudication loop for a Friday installation was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: access modes, permissions, publishing authority, spending/usage bounds and goal text arrive as user-set constraints or constructor safeguards rather than deliberated organization policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the person establishes the goal, access level, budgets, and final commit/PR/deployment approval; model-driven workers cannot legitimize a change of identity or waive these user boundaries. - Decision owner: external local user/owner and platform permission constraints; no first-party S5 policy-maker with a demonstrated return to the running organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Crew read/ask/full permissions, src/team-budget.ts, approval events, self-run prohibitions on default-branch push/deployment/data deletion/spending, manual final review. - Closure path: human chooses constraints → Friday enforces them during tasks → agent operates in the bounded space; no question of ultimate mission/identity is assessed by an S5 authority and promulgated as a legitimate new policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous lead controlling work is S3, not by itself a constitutional S5, and static access/approval settings are control tools rather than a policy arbitration loop. - Evidence: pinned README human review/Crew settings, src/teammates.ts, src/team-selfrun.ts, src/team-budget.ts, src/runner/runner.ts. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human operator or organization may exercise S5 outside the shipped runtime; such parent authority is not silently imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d1c43df574e84c7b5d9a3e8b68ab4ea0be83d739/assessments/fr/friday.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d1c43df574e84c7b5d9a3e8b68ab4ea0be83d739\">d1c43df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:26:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/one-king-studio/hildi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hildi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/one-king-studio/hildi/tree/d5fb376ab918e02facdbdcc0fbf00dabb3f6a806\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d5fb376ab918e02facdbdcc0fbf00dabb3f6a806\">d5fb376 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-led coding and file/system task execution through observed tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User instructions, unknown file state, command errors, provider tool-call responses and subtask results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tools/arguments, decide edits and shell commands, consult source, request child work and revise steps based on returned observations. - Decision owner: The model in each supported session/child agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TAgentLoop, OpenAI-compatible model transport, approval gate, command classification, SQLite session store, socket runtime and bounded tools. - Closure path: User/ambient context → LLM tool selection → execution under approval → tool result returned in conversation → subsequent model turn or final answer; child completions may wake the parent for another turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipped hildi-agent runtime constructs THarnessSession with TAgentLoop and tool registry; the TUI drives the same socket session and child processes run the same agent mechanism. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic approval/security and session persistence remain without a model, but they cannot choose which code changes solve the current task. - Evidence: HildiAgent, HildiHarnessSession tool wiring, HildiSessionRuntime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high in wired agent/tool loop; provider efficacy not exercised. - Caveats: Headless model inference requires a configured external provider; source-level claims are not live model task benchmarks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Suppress duplicate work and stale-state oscillation among independent model-operated sessions in the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two peers simultaneously starting the same task, overwriting one another&#39;s workspace changes or acting on stale task/board state. - Distinct S1 units: Separate independently started live agent sessions detected via sessionruntimes, explicitly distinguished from parent-owned spawned child sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing peers race to claim an open card; without the guarded claim both could work it and issue conflicting changes. Protocol also identifies path ownership/commit cadence as conflict risks. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared board decisions plus taskclaim&#39;s atomic compare-and-swap, holder/age refusal and mandatory claim-before-change rule; peer mode is selected from actual live runtime presence rather than a static multi-agent flag. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Losing claim returns holder/status and how long held to the requesting model; board notices and mode-transition ambient messages prompt a fresh boardread, revised claim/task choice or peer protocol. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Conflict arises between distinct independent S1 agents; a first-party prevention gate identifies its winner/loser, and the model-directed protocol uses that feedback to adjust later operations. Spawned children are deliberately treated as delegation, not peer negotiation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a different card, defer modifications, negotiate path ownership/commit protocol and record a new shared decision when contention or changing peer presence is observed. - Decision owner: Each model-driven peer agent, with deterministic SQLite CAS as enforcement, not as discretionary coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HildiCoordination live-peer query and prompt, TSqliteTaskStore.Claim, TTaskTool.ClaimCard, boardpost/read, task update and stale-source warnings. - Closure path: Contending work intentions → atomic claim/board visibility → successful claim or explicit holder refusal → returned tool/ambient feedback → agent revises own next task and writes a shared coordination decision when needed. - Boundary reachability: A stored workspace session wires TTaskTool and TBoardTools directly into the model registry; DetectCoordinationMode and NoticeBoard run in the supported per-turn session path with peer joining/leaving messages. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The CAS cannot decide which alternative task, path ownership or coordination protocol to adopt after conflict; the model session owns that discretion. - Evidence: HildiCoordination, ClaimCard, atomic Claim, NoticeBoard and tool registration. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for peer-level claim contention; not evidence of an always-successful multi-agent planning consensus. - Caveats: Claim-before-change is an instruction, not a forced path-level reservation for every write; external tools may bypass it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Maintain and intervene in current child-agent commitments in a parent-session work portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Active child availability, asynchronous failures, task ownership changes, approval stalls and urgent operator takeover during live work. - Whole-system current view: The model obtains child status/slot acknowledgments and ambient terminal results, while the human operator&#39;s sidebar/spy shows the full current child roster and direct child transcript/live stream in the same parent composition. - Current-control decision scope: Model chooses delegate/message/abort/release actions, task scope, child follow-ups and parent next work; human chooses abort/release/promote of a specific child with live roster context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the base mode, the parent model allocates/reallocates work and responds to child outcomes; in the supervised mode, the operator decides whether a live child remains under the parent&#39;s current control. - Decision owner: Base A mode — parent model; parent P mode — legitimate operator acting through the TUI for that task composition. SubagentSupervisor only enforces capacities, socket commands, event relay and lineage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child process supervisor, max/depth caps, message/abort/release command handlers, parent prompt queue, child roster, observer socket, status board and session lineage detachment. - Closure path: Base: delegation → independent child run → result/aborted/gone event → ambient parent wake → model changes next commitments. Parent mode: operator inspects roster/live child → aborts/releases/promotes → runtime command changes child operation/lineage, and takeover coordination notice reaches the former parent. - Boundary reachability: HildiSessionRuntime creates TSubagentSupervisor and registers the model-callable child tools; TUI ShowSpy, OnSpyAction and AnnounceTakeover are shipped with the sibling client and address the live parent/child session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The base choice of which task to delegate or revise is model discretionary; the optional human takeover is an independently evidenced alternative decisive owner. Registry limits and event loops enforce but do not select either owner&#39;s intervention. - Evidence: subagent tools and service, supervisor, runtime ambient return, TUI takeover path. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for delegated current-control and observed parent mode, bounded to one composition. - Caveats: This is not a whole-organization manager of all unrelated peer sessions. The P mode is optional and its board notification is best-effort; loss of the notification can weaken parent awareness although lineage detachment still changes what the parent can command.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Supply an independent-from-summary observation path into a delegated child&#39;s actual conversation/tool operations for potential corrective audit by an operator or composed reviewer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parent receives only a summarized child result, which may hide failed tool actions or unexpectedly broad work; a distinct observer can inspect recorded tool/action reality. - Claim being audited: Whether a child actually performed the stated actions and whether its work should continue under parent authority. - Ordinary reporting path: TSubagentSupervisor sends summarized completion/status as an ambient result to the parent model. - Complementary access path: Separate observe-only TSessionClient socket to the child&#39;s runtime plus a stored per-child transcript tail, showing raw tool, error, assistant and live event stream. - Independence boundary: The observer is not the child&#39;s own summary and does not gain a drive role; it reads first-party child runtime/store evidence directly. It is not independent of the physical host&#39;s persistence or evidence integrity, and does not itself make an audit verdict. - Who acts on findings: In the shipped UI a human can inspect and abort/promote/release a child, with takeover notice and detachment; a composed independent audit judgment/mandate remains required for autonomous S3 ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare direct child execution evidence with reported status and decide whether/how to return corrective intervention; the specific observation/intervention seam exists, but no dedicated independent autonomous reviewer is wired. - Decision owner: Potential human auditor/constructor consumer; no shipped agent-owned S3 judgment. The TUI supplies direct access and intervention construction points, not an automatic auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only session observer, historical message tail, live stream renderer, child status, abort/release/promote controls and board takeover status. - Closure path: Parent result claim → optional direct child spy and transcript examination → operator may intervene via runtime command and parent board notice; an autonomous audit actor and systematic checking policy would require composition. - Boundary reachability: Sidebar/ShowSpy and TSubagentSpy.Open are shipped UI paths; OnSpyAction calls the supported runtime command and AnnounceTakeover publishes a corrective status for the former parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second direct observer and corrective control surfaces are not just the ordinary result summary, but no first-party model auditor decides independent findings; accordingly the scoped S3-specific constructor is C, not A. - Evidence: HildiHarnessSubagentSpy, TUI ShowSpy / OnSpyAction, runtime ordinary child reporting. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; the claim is deliberately limited to complementary observation and possible corrective return, not guaranteed independent audit. - Caveats: The user&#39;s decision may be ordinary supervision rather than a formal audit; an observed and acted-on discrepancy is not demonstrated by a live trace in this review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No separately closed process that evaluates external future possibilities and adapts the workspace organization or operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing providers, project context and tool availability may require new skills or working strategy, but the installed agent mostly responds within the current task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge external prospective capability needs and return a strategic adaptation into present operation; no such closed first-party decision owner is installed. - Decision owner: User/developer selects skills, MCP providers and runtime upgrades; a model chooses on-demand current tools, board entries and task plans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP tool reconciliation, dynamic prompt assembly, skill loading, checkpoint/handoff, workspace board, goals and current task queue. - Closure path: Tools/skills can change with configuration and context checkpoints can carry a recent summary; none supplies a future-oriented outside assessment and authoritative capability-adaptation decision returned to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A static skill or saved plan may shape next work, but neither is a prospective adaptation regulator with continuing responsibility. - Evidence: turn assembly, skills, MCP provider, feature statuses. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded shipped modes. - Caveats: An external agent might use Hildi to maintain/evolve a project, but such an additional organization&#39;s strategic loop is not Hildi&#39;s standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No complete runtime constitutional-identity or ultimate-policy adjudication and return loop at the assessed agent organization level. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky shell actions, approval scopes, task-card moderation and peer protocol changes remain local work/coordination decisions rather than identity-level constitutional conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively resolve a conflict about organizational identity or ultimate purpose, then return a binding policy decision to all affected ongoing operation; not present. - Decision owner: Operator grants tool permissions and moderates shared records; model agents negotiate path/task protocols; maintainers decide repository policy outside the shipped runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Command effect classification and approval, shared board moderation, operator task reassignment and peer coordination prompt. - Closure path: User approval allows/denies tools and peer board decisions guide later tasks, but no identity-level matter → legitimate ultimate-policy authority → binding return to the live organization is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Claim ownership, operator takeover and shared protocol negotiation affect present work allocation (S2/S3), not the durable ultimate purpose or identity of the system. - Evidence: approval, board tools, task moderation, coordination protocol. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: The public project&#39;s actual maintainer identity/governance decisions are adjacent, not a governing organ reached through the live harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/63dd717d3de738410edbc0ef7a65853126bb511c/assessments/hi/hildi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 63dd717d3de738410edbc0ef7a65853126bb511c\">63dd717 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
